00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.310
Before we get
started today, I just

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:13.020
did want to make a couple of
announcements about homework

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:14.820
because just a bunch
of things came up

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.190
that I thought it's
probably better for me

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:18.910
to say something about.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:23.010
So the first is, we had a
couple of people inquire

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:24.570
about our solutions, right?

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:27.330
And so our solutions
to the homework

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:30.610
are generally much more involved
than we would expect from you.

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:34.950
In fact, we wouldn't even want
you to do that because it's not

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.210
the point, right?

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:38.460
In this class, we don't care
about little corner cases

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.500
and things like
that, we don't care.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.440
But we write solutions, usually,
that are a bit more involved.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.362
In fact, a lot of
the solutions are

00:00:45.362 --> 00:00:47.070
for things where we
initially didn't even

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.110
ask for justification, right?

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:54.060
So we're just writing-- so our
solutions, we're writing just--

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.330
they might not quite pass
muster in the math department,

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:01.170
but they'd be awfully close,
and we are absolutely not

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:02.370
expecting that of you.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.837
So for people who look at the
solutions and go, like, whoa,

00:01:05.837 --> 00:01:07.420
like, that's a long
solution, and what

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:08.545
are all these corner cases?

00:01:08.545 --> 00:01:09.700
You don't have to do that.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:10.200
OK.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:14.530
So that's the first thing,
just to reassure you.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:17.770
CVXPY had some questions.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:21.730
So I just want to say, make
one announcement about this,

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:26.380
whatever you do, do not
treat the coding assignments

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:29.330
where you do, CVXPY, as kind
of at a monkey at a typewriter

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:29.830
thing.

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:31.930
Like, type stuff in and
say, oh, it doesn't work.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:35.260
It doesn't-- and then
conceptualize it as, I have

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:38.530
to find out a way for CVXPY
to accept what I'm saying.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.970
This is exactly the
opposite of what we want.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:42.730
And if we detect
that at any time,

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:45.160
it's going to piss us off.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:48.250
And bad things happen
when we get pissed off.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:50.750
So anyway, it's not the point.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:56.050
So the main point is this, in
this one convex programming you

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:57.520
know what you're doing, right?

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.560
So if you type in--

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.900
if you type in something
that's non-convex,

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.480
and then post something
saying, I don't know,

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:08.169
CVXPY doesn't accept
my something squared

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:10.720
equals 3 constraint.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:13.150
That is uncool,
that's all I'm going

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:15.430
to say, at least in
this class, very uncool.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:18.310
So I'm just saying,
it's not the right way

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:20.560
to do a programming
class, and it's definitely

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.720
not the right way to
approach these problems.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:26.187
So, I mean, it'd perfectly OK
if you have some syntax thing

00:02:26.187 --> 00:02:26.770
or something--

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:28.870
I mean, everyone at
some point in their life

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:31.120
is going to declare
a variable x.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:34.960
Later, think that x is
actually the numerical vector

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:36.280
after you've solved it.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:38.410
Plug-in x somewhere
and it's going to say,

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:39.730
what are you doing?

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:43.780
Python will, because well,
x is an object, and x.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:45.010
value is what you want.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:46.810
Everybody understand
what I'm saying?

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:51.850
Everyone will confuse an equals
with equals equals, right?

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:54.640
Equals is actually an
assignment in Python.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:55.840
Right?

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:01.750
Equals equals is actually a
special thing in Python if it--

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:06.940
sorry, in CVXPY, if it occurs
between two CVXPY expressions

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:11.860
that is over-- so expression
equals equals expression is

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:13.870
overloaded to be a constraint.

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:15.850
And it is only a--

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:18.940
you can form it all you like,
but you can't solve a problem

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:21.363
with it unless both the
left and right hand sides

00:03:21.363 --> 00:03:22.030
are [INAUDIBLE].

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:23.280
Everybody see what I'm saying?

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:24.770
So I'm just saying, please--

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:28.450
I know most of you--
but please, just make

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:31.390
sure you're present thinking
about what you're doing

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:33.190
and not just typing
stuff in, right?

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:35.360
So that's-- because that's
not the spirit of it, OK?

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:35.860
All right.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:39.500
That was weird but
I needed to say it.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:41.900
Oh, the other
question was about,

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:45.290
we start doing some of these
kind of practical-ish problems,

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:47.120
actually some of
them are quite--

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:48.830
are actually quite real.

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:51.140
I mean, real versions of
them would be not much more

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:52.370
