what I'm saying there.

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:50.360
So people say the Lagrange
dual, but the truth

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:53.330
is when people talk
about duals, you

00:10:53.330 --> 00:10:56.210
have license to make a
few small transformations

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:57.500
of your problem.

00:10:57.500 --> 00:10:59.360
And they still-- and
actually, sometimes you

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:01.730
can do different ones and
then you get different duals

00:11:01.730 --> 00:11:03.380
and they have people's names.

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:06.710
Like you could talk about
the Clark dual of a QP,

00:11:06.710 --> 00:11:09.950
or the Frank dual, and
they're slightly different.

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:13.550
And that has to do with
people doing small--

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:16.350
making small changes
equivalence--

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:18.350
making an equivalence
transformation then taking

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:19.550
a dual or afterwards.

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:21.250
OK.

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:27.310
And then this one would have
a dual right here, like that.

00:11:27.310 --> 00:11:28.960
I'll just write it this way.

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:31.270
Like that, tilde, right?

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:35.440
And so the question is,
how are these related?

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:38.650
And you know, I said, that
if you're trained in math,

00:11:38.650 --> 00:11:40.540
you will have an
overwhelming urge

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:43.000
to draw an arrow here
and say that these

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:45.190
are related in some way,
or something like that.

00:11:45.190 --> 00:11:48.820
I mean they're obviously
related, because these two,

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:49.930
they are--

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:53.860
each one is the dual of two
problems that are equivalent.

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:54.490
Right?

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:57.310
So assuming strong duality
holds, which we will simply

00:11:57.310 --> 00:11:58.660
assume from now on without--

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:01.060
I won't say anything
else because this

00:12:01.060 --> 00:12:02.230
isn't a math class.

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:03.850
I would say-- then
you'd say, look,

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:06.470
they have the same optimal value
and all that sort of stuff.

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:06.970
Right?

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:08.620
So OK.

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:11.660
It turns out this is
just not the case.

00:12:11.660 --> 00:12:13.960
And we'll see incredibly
simple examples

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:17.500
where transformations give
you radically different duals.

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:21.020
OK, so that's-- which is
actually kind of interesting.

00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:21.520
All right.

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:25.540
So let's start with
the following problem.

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:27.220
So here's one.

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:31.720
Let's minimize a function of
an affine function, right?

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:33.670
And f 0 is convex, right?

00:12:33.670 --> 00:12:35.515
So then-- oh, yeah.

00:12:35.515 --> 00:12:37.390
By the way, we didn't
get to talk about that.

00:12:37.390 --> 00:12:40.510
We didn't get to-- let's
cover the theory of Lagrange

00:12:40.510 --> 00:12:43.910
duality for problems
with no constraints.

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:44.410
Right?

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:46.930
So OK, so what's the Lagrangian?

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:49.240
Well, there are no
constraints so there's nothing

00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:50.360
to add to the objective.

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:53.200
So the Lagrangian is
nothing but the objective.

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:54.100
OK?

00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:56.320
Then it says, OK
that's fine, and now

00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:58.240
let's find the dual function.

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:00.642
Well the dual
function of the no--

00:13:00.642 --> 00:13:03.100
there are no dual variables
because there's no constraints,

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:05.830
but it's a dual
function and it says you

00:13:05.830 --> 00:13:07.570
should minimize this objective.

00:13:07.570 --> 00:13:09.890
Anyway, when you do
that, you get p star.

00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:10.390
OK?

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:11.950
And then this is--

00:13:11.950 --> 00:13:12.770
OK.

00:13:12.770 --> 00:13:15.250
By the way, that--
it's correct, right?

00:13:15.250 --> 00:13:16.390
Number one, let's observe.

00:13:16.390 --> 00:13:20.210
It's a lower bound on p star
and you have to admit that.

00:13:20.210 --> 00:13:23.630
It's also and completely and
utterly useless because someone

00:13:23.630 --> 00:13:25.310
says to me, help me
solve this problem.

00:13:25.310 --> 00:13:26.960
Actually, what I really
need is a lower bound

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:28.310
on the upper value
and you go, I got

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:29.600
a great lower bound for you.

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:30.950
And you go, what?

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:32.750
The optimal value, right?

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:35.630
And it's like, there's
several jokes where

00:13:35.630 --> 00:13:37.730
the punchline is like that.

00:13:37.730 --> 00:13:39.710
Those people had to
have been mathematicians

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:42.860
and some people say
why, and they say,

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:44.540
oh because the
answer was precisely

00:13:44.540 --> 00:13:46.590
correct and totally useless.

00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:47.090
Right?

00:13:47.090 --> 00:13:48.620
So has anyone heard these?

00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:49.460
I'm not going to--

00:13:49.460 --> 00:13:50.210
you've heard them.

00:13:50.210 --> 00:13:52.140
I'm not going to reproduce
because they're all terrible.

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:53.307
Anyway, but that's the idea.

00:13:53.307 --> 00:13:54.320
So that's what this is.

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:55.880
Right?

00:13:55.880 --> 00:13:57.620
OK.

00:13:57.620 --> 00:14:00.482
So now let's make an
innocent transfer--

00:14:00.482 --> 00:14:02.690
we're going to make an
innocent transformation from p

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:03.268
to p tilde.

00:14:03.268 --> 00:14:05.060
And the way we're going
to do that is we're

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:07.370
going to introduce equal--

00:14:07.370 --> 00:14:08.530
introduce new variables.

00:14:08.530 --> 00:14:10.280
So we're going to give
this a name, that's

00:14:10.280 --> 00:14:13.140
going to be called y, and we're
going to write this problem--

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:15.950
which is-- this is obviously
equivalent to that.

00:14:15.950 --> 00:14:18.410
So that's our p and our p tilde.

00:14:18.410 --> 00:14:19.400
This is p tilde.

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:21.500
And now when I work
out the dual of this,

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:23.090
I get something that
looks like that.

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:26.730
And it's highly--
it's not useless.

00:14:26.730 --> 00:14:29.030
It's interesting-- it's
actually an interesting dual.

00:14:29.030 --> 00:14:30.350
Right?

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:34.560
And I won't-- this is a quick
calculation to show that

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:39.300
the dual of this is-- if I
minimize f 0 plus nu transpose

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:43.530
times this thing, I can express
that in terms of the conjugate

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:44.560
of the function.

00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:45.060
Right?

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:46.650
So OK.

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:49.030
So this is kind of
a dramatic example,

00:14:49.030 --> 00:14:51.270
saying that even
the most simple,

00:14:51.270 --> 00:14:53.670
innocent-looking transform--

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:55.920
if you apply an innocent
transformation ahead of time,

00:14:55.920 --> 00:14:59.640
you get a radically
different dual, including,

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:01.320
in the first case,
you get one that's

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:03.460
just utterly and completely
useless and the second case,

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:04.230
you actually get
something that's

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:05.730
interest-- that actually
tells you something.

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:06.230
Right?

00:15:06.230 --> 00:15:09.450
So OK.

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:12.030
We can look at another
one just for fun.

00:15:12.030 --> 00:15:14.188
Is-- we'll look at the
normal approximation

00:15:14.188 --> 00:15:16.230
problem which we're going
to look at later today.

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:20.160
So that's choosing x to
minimize norm ax minus b.

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:22.380
Again, it's kind
of silly to talk

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:25.240
about the Lagrange dual of a
problem with no constraints,

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:25.740
right?

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:28.560
So in this case, we
introduce constraints,

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:30.030
and the constraints
we introduce,

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:32.910
we give a name for ax minus b,
and you get this kind of thing,

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:35.770
and you end up with a
very interesting thing.

00:15:35.770 --> 00:15:36.270
Right?

00:15:36.270 --> 00:15:38.010
This is the norm.

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:40.030
By the way, I could
take the norm squared

00:15:40.030 --> 00:15:43.020
and I would get
a different dual.

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:43.620
Right?

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:47.950
So I get a completely different
dual if I took the square.

00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:51.890
I'm not going to do it, but
you might check into that.

00:15:51.890 --> 00:15:56.180
And the dual, when you work this
out, is this is the norm plus--

00:15:56.180 --> 00:15:58.860
then there's a constant over
here, that's irrelevant,

00:15:58.860 --> 00:16:00.920
and then this
whole thing here is

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:03.440
a linear function of x and y.

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:07.400
And if I look at y over
here, and I minimize

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:10.400
this over y, what I get
is the indicator function

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:11.340
of the dual norm.

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:11.840
Right?

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:16.430
So you end up with something
that looks like this.

00:16:16.430 --> 00:16:21.450
And so the dual, or I should
say, like a dual of-- in fact,

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:23.990
some people would even say a
dual of that problem is this,

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:26.540
and then someone would say,
no the dual of that problem

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:27.540
is just silly.

00:16:27.540 --> 00:16:30.650
It's maximized p star over
no dual variables, right?

00:16:30.650 --> 00:16:33.290
Which is kind of goofy.

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:36.170
But in fact, people would
allow you this license

00:16:36.170 --> 00:16:37.820
and you get something here.

00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:39.320
By the way, by
now, you should be

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:42.470
starting to see patterns
which should be--

00:16:42.470 --> 00:16:45.140
you know, when you
see a function appear

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:48.200
in what's called the primal
problem, then in the dual,

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:51.120
you should expect
to see conjugates.

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:53.650
So if your original
function, for example,

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:55.770
had like Kullback-Leibler
divergence,

00:16:55.770 --> 00:17:00.150
or relative entropy, then
you should-- even before you

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:01.890
start doing any
calculation, you should

00:17:01.890 --> 00:17:07.050
expect to see exponentials
and log sum x in the dual.

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:09.884
And if it doesn't show up that
way, then that's kind of-- you

00:17:09.884 --> 00:17:11.550
should maybe double
check what you did.

00:17:11.550 --> 00:17:14.410
Everybody see what I'm saying?

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:16.290
So here, the other
thing is, there's

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:18.089
some other obvious
things you should know--

00:17:18.089 --> 00:17:22.050
I mean, you should expect to see
the adjoint or the transpose.

00:17:22.050 --> 00:17:24.750
If you see an a here,
you should not at all

00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:27.210
be surprised to see an a
transpose appear in the dual.

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:28.420
OK, so I won't--

00:17:28.420 --> 00:17:31.230
some people make tables
of this about what

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:33.030
you expect to see
in one or the other

00:17:33.030 --> 00:17:34.885
and all that kind of stuff.

00:17:34.885 --> 00:17:36.510
I think it's better
just to think of it

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:39.900
as patterns that
you should expect,

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:43.140
and you should-- so you
should just be preloading.

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:45.670
There should be things you
should expect all of us--

00:17:45.670 --> 00:17:49.020
If your problem involves
l-infinity norms,

00:17:49.020 --> 00:17:51.165
the dual should
probably involve?

00:17:55.330 --> 00:17:58.380
Conjugate of an l infinity
norm or not conjugate, what?

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:01.310
You should get an L. You should
expect an L1 norm, exactly.

00:18:01.310 --> 00:18:01.810
Right?

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:04.490
So it's just-- it's
stuff like that.

00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:05.815
These are things that--

00:18:05.815 --> 00:18:07.085
you will see patterns.

00:18:07.085 --> 00:18:07.585
OK?

00:18:12.220 --> 00:18:16.480
This is what people refer to
as something called partial--

00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:20.320
the partial dualization
or partial Lagrangian.

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:22.120
What you do is--

00:18:22.120 --> 00:18:24.570
here's an original problem.

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:28.250
And if you work
out, this is an LP,

00:18:28.250 --> 00:18:29.750
and when you work
out the LP you get

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:31.470
something that looks like that.

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:31.970
OK?

00:18:31.970 --> 00:18:33.620
The dual of that LP, right?

00:18:33.620 --> 00:18:36.230
So another way to do it
is to say, you know what?

00:18:36.230 --> 00:18:38.660
I am not going to
dualize over this.

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:40.730
People call that a
box constraint right?

00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:42.470
It's a constraint that
all your variables

00:18:42.470 --> 00:18:43.640
are between plus and minus 1.

00:18:43.640 --> 00:18:45.390
You see, I'm not going
to dualize over it.

00:18:45.390 --> 00:18:47.360
Instead, I'm going
to take this thing,

00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:49.820
and I'm going to make an
indicator function of it,

00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:53.180
and plug it into c trans--
into the objective, right?

00:18:53.180 --> 00:18:55.130
So obviously, it's equivalent.

00:18:55.130 --> 00:18:58.190
So this problem is clearly
equivalent to this one.

00:18:58.190 --> 00:18:58.790
Right?

00:18:58.790 --> 00:19:01.130
The difference is here
you would say that the box

00:19:01.130 --> 00:19:02.840
constraints are explicit.

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:06.680
Here, the box constraints
are implicit because they're

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:08.570
embedded in the
objective and it just

00:19:08.570 --> 00:19:10.820
makes the objective
return plus infinity

00:19:10.820 --> 00:19:12.290
if you're not feasible.

00:19:12.290 --> 00:19:12.980
Right?

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:14.010
Make sense?

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:15.170
So, all right.

00:19:15.170 --> 00:19:18.750
Well, we can actually work
out Lagrange dual of this.

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:23.930
So here, remember, what happens
is you form a Lagrangian.

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:27.510
The Lagrangian is actually
an affine function of x.

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:28.680
Then you minimize.

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:30.690
But when you minimize
an affine function,

00:19:30.690 --> 00:19:33.720
you just either
get minus infinity,

00:19:33.720 --> 00:19:35.820
or you get the
constant part, but only

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:38.010
when the linear
part is 0, right?

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:40.290
Here, it's different
because here I

00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:45.330
have to minimize here
an affine function of x

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:48.630
subject to all the entries
between minus 1 and 1.

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:50.340
That we can do
analytically, right?

00:19:50.340 --> 00:19:53.490
Because the constant
part, that's

00:19:53.490 --> 00:19:55.860
nu transpose minus
b that pops out,

00:19:55.860 --> 00:19:58.530
and then here you end
up with a linear thing,

00:19:58.530 --> 00:20:02.640
a linear function of x, it is
in fact a transpose mu plus c,

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:04.410
quantity, transpose x.

00:20:04.410 --> 00:20:07.350
And we want to minimize
that over all x's

00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:08.700
between minus 1 and 1.

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:11.123
And this is like
Cauchy-Schwartz I guess,

00:20:11.123 --> 00:20:12.540
it's called the
Holder inequality.

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:15.750
And the hole-- that says
the best choice is to take--

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:20.230
you look at the entries of this
vector whenever it is positive,

00:20:20.230 --> 00:20:22.283
you take x as minus
1, xi is minus 1,

00:20:22.283 --> 00:20:23.700
and whenever an
entry is negative,

00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:25.230
you take xi equals plus 1.

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:27.870
That will minimize
and the minimum value

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:30.750
is going to be the sum
of the absolute values

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:31.840
with a negative sign.

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:33.030
And so you get this.

00:20:33.030 --> 00:20:37.560
And it's kind of cool, and so
you could even say this box--

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:41.370
so other people would say
this box constrained problem

00:20:41.370 --> 00:20:43.320
has this simple dual, here.

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:46.260
And it kind of everything's
matching up, right?

00:20:46.260 --> 00:20:48.235
That-- you can think of this--

00:20:48.235 --> 00:20:49.860
you could have thought
of this as the l

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:53.040
infinity norm of
x is less than 1.

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:56.010
So then using those
weird vague rules

00:20:56.010 --> 00:20:58.440
I said before, you see
an l infinity norm,

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:00.450
you should hardly be
surprised if in the dual

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:01.740
an L1 norm comes up.

00:21:01.740 --> 00:21:03.900
And sure enough, it did.

00:21:03.900 --> 00:21:04.410
OK?

00:21:04.410 --> 00:21:06.960
So makes sense?

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:10.260
Then an interesting
thing is it turns out

00:21:10.260 --> 00:21:12.870
this is a case where
there is a nice bar here.

00:21:12.870 --> 00:21:16.860
Because in fact, if you
stare at this long enough,

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:19.020
you will see something
really interesting.

00:21:19.020 --> 00:21:21.420
Lambda 1 and lambda 2
are both non-negative.

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:25.320
Their difference is equal
to c plus a transpose nu.

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:28.260
And you're asked to
minimize-- to maximize

00:21:28.260 --> 00:21:32.280
the negative sum of the
lambda 1 minus the lambda 2s.

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:34.590
When you look at that
closely and stare at it,

00:21:34.590 --> 00:21:37.110
you'll realize you know
precisely what that is.

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:40.720
This, the optimal value of
this thing, is exactly that.

00:21:40.720 --> 00:21:43.260
And in fact, if you were to
write-- express this as an LP,

00:21:43.260 --> 00:21:44.250
it would be that.

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:50.220
So this is a case where d
and d tilde happen to be

00:21:50.220 --> 00:21:51.900
extremely closely related.

00:21:51.900 --> 00:21:52.470
Right?

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:53.710
So, OK?

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:54.700
Make sense?

00:21:59.010 --> 00:22:01.620
Last topic, and then I'm going
to mention one more thing.

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:04.240
Is problems with
generalized inequalities.

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:06.570
So remember what this means
that what we're going to do

00:22:06.570 --> 00:22:08.820
is we're going to have an
inequality constraint where

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:10.410
that's a vector.

00:22:10.410 --> 00:22:11.610
And you have a--

00:22:11.610 --> 00:22:13.200
and the constraint
is now that instead

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:15.570
of being less than or
equal to 0 as a scalar,

00:22:15.570 --> 00:22:17.110
it's actually like a vector.

00:22:17.110 --> 00:22:18.960
So I mean it could
be, for example, this

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:20.400
could be a matrix,
and this could

00:22:20.400 --> 00:22:24.450
mean that the matrix is negative
semidefinite, for example.

00:22:24.450 --> 00:22:25.050
Right?

00:22:25.050 --> 00:22:27.210
I mean, more often
it would be a matrix

00:22:27.210 --> 00:22:31.720
is positive semidefinite, but
anyway, something like that.

00:22:31.720 --> 00:22:34.830
So here, it turns out
everything just kind

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:39.120
of works, which means that
all the notation was good.

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:44.070
So here's what you do, is
you form a Lagrangian by--

00:22:44.070 --> 00:22:45.850
the equality constraints
don't change.

00:22:45.850 --> 00:22:47.640
But for the inequality
constraints,

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:48.818
we do the same thing.

00:22:48.818 --> 00:22:50.610
Remember if this was
scalar, you would just

00:22:50.610 --> 00:22:54.207
say lambda i times f i Lambda
i is bigger than or equal to 0,

00:22:54.207 --> 00:22:56.790
and remember the constraint is
f i is less than or equal to 0,

00:22:56.790 --> 00:22:58.910
but now they're vectors.

00:22:58.910 --> 00:22:59.510
Right?

00:22:59.510 --> 00:23:03.200
So it turns out what you want
is to form this inner product

00:23:03.200 --> 00:23:07.790
and what it's going to turn
out is that lambda i should

00:23:07.790 --> 00:23:09.680
be actually--

00:23:09.680 --> 00:23:15.630
lambda i is going to have to be
non-negative in the dual cone.

00:23:15.630 --> 00:23:16.130
Right?

00:23:16.130 --> 00:23:18.290
Which, again, kind
of makes sense here.

00:23:18.290 --> 00:23:18.860
Right?

00:23:18.860 --> 00:23:26.240
So here's the Lagrangian, and
then here's the dual function.

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:28.460
And then the lower
bound property

00:23:28.460 --> 00:23:30.260
is the same, right,
because what happens

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:33.980
is if these vectors are bigger--

00:23:33.980 --> 00:23:38.450
if they're bigger and equal to 0
in this generalized inequality,

00:23:38.450 --> 00:23:43.860
then it turns out that dual
function is a lower bound on p

00:23:43.860 --> 00:23:44.360
star.

00:23:44.360 --> 00:23:44.860
Right?

00:23:44.860 --> 00:23:47.900
And the way you do that is it's
identical to the other one,

00:23:47.900 --> 00:23:50.690
and the other one there's no
transpose lambda i is a scalar,

00:23:50.690 --> 00:23:54.140
f i is a scalar, and we use
the deep fact, in that case,

00:23:54.140 --> 00:23:56.540
that the product of
a non-negative number

00:23:56.540 --> 00:23:59.120
and a non-positive
number is non-positive.

00:23:59.120 --> 00:24:02.330
Here, it is a little bit more.

00:24:02.330 --> 00:24:05.240
We're using the fact that if
I give you a non-negative--

00:24:05.240 --> 00:24:08.880
a vector that's non-negative
in the dual cone,

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:11.965
and the inner product of
that with a vector that is--

00:24:11.965 --> 00:24:12.840
did I get that right?

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:16.890
Yes, non-positive in
another cone, in the cone,

00:24:16.890 --> 00:24:19.080
then the inner
product is actually

00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:21.000
less than or equal to 0.

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:23.640
So it's not much more
complicated, but that's it.

00:24:23.640 --> 00:24:24.270
Right?

00:24:24.270 --> 00:24:26.280
And then you get a dual
problem and everything

00:24:26.280 --> 00:24:27.960
works later, everything.

00:24:27.960 --> 00:24:29.910
Everything just works.

00:24:29.910 --> 00:24:32.847
And we'll look at just
the last example of this.

00:24:32.847 --> 00:24:34.680
But it's kind of cool,
because this is stuff

00:24:34.680 --> 00:24:38.050
that I don't know, 25 years ago,
30 years ago, no one knew this.

00:24:38.050 --> 00:24:39.300
Would have been just stunning.

00:24:39.300 --> 00:24:42.660
But here it is.

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:44.340
Here's a semidefinite program.

00:24:44.340 --> 00:24:46.240
You're minimizing
a linear function.

00:24:46.240 --> 00:24:48.180
I get-- maybe this
is an inequality

00:24:48.180 --> 00:24:49.740
form, subject to--
this is called

00:24:49.740 --> 00:24:51.933
an LMI, a Linear
Matrix Inequality.

00:24:51.933 --> 00:24:53.850
It's supposed to look
like a linear inequality

00:24:53.850 --> 00:24:56.490
except these are
symmetric matrices.

00:24:56.490 --> 00:24:59.220
The f's and the g,
and that inequality

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:02.530
there is with respect to the
positive semidefinite cone.

00:25:02.530 --> 00:25:03.340
OK?

00:25:03.340 --> 00:25:05.690
Then the Lagrangian
looks like this.

00:25:05.690 --> 00:25:07.570
So here I have to
take the inner product

00:25:07.570 --> 00:25:08.897
of what I would call lambda.

00:25:08.897 --> 00:25:10.480
Here I'm going to--
instead of lambda,

00:25:10.480 --> 00:25:12.460
I'm going to call
it capital Z. It's

00:25:12.460 --> 00:25:14.830
going to be a symmetric
matrix, and you

00:25:14.830 --> 00:25:17.680
should read trace
of Z times that's

00:25:17.680 --> 00:25:21.610
the error, or this minus that.

00:25:21.610 --> 00:25:23.510
You should read that
as the inner product.

00:25:23.510 --> 00:25:30.530
So this is basically what
lambda transpose f i or f 1 of x

00:25:30.530 --> 00:25:31.030
would be.

00:25:31.030 --> 00:25:33.520
That's how you should
read this part.

00:25:33.520 --> 00:25:36.160
And here's the
Lagrangian, and the dual

00:25:36.160 --> 00:25:38.740
says, I should
minimize this over x.

00:25:38.740 --> 00:25:42.040
I mean, it's complicated because
these are symmetric matrices

00:25:42.040 --> 00:25:44.800
flying all over the place.

00:25:44.800 --> 00:25:47.590
But it turns out, this is
simply an affine function

00:25:47.590 --> 00:25:49.420
of x and the same story holds.

00:25:49.420 --> 00:25:49.990
Right?

00:25:49.990 --> 00:25:52.450
If you minimize an
affine function,

00:25:52.450 --> 00:25:55.930
it's going to be minus
infinity unless the linear part

00:25:55.930 --> 00:25:56.560
vanishes.

00:25:56.560 --> 00:25:57.060
Right?

00:25:57.060 --> 00:26:00.580
And so if it does, it says--

00:26:00.580 --> 00:26:04.280
if I look at how does
this depend on x 1?

00:26:04.280 --> 00:26:08.780
And the answer is it's c 1
x 1, that's from here, plus

00:26:08.780 --> 00:26:11.840
and then trace Z f 1 times x 1.

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:14.420
And so those two added
together have to be 0.

00:26:14.420 --> 00:26:16.820
Otherwise, if I
take x 1 going off

00:26:16.820 --> 00:26:18.800
to plus infinity
or minus infinity,

00:26:18.800 --> 00:26:22.010
I can make this thing
minus infinity, right?

00:26:22.010 --> 00:26:23.100
So you get this.

00:26:23.100 --> 00:26:26.150
And then you end up with this
dual semidefinite program

00:26:26.150 --> 00:26:27.110
and it looks like that.

00:26:27.110 --> 00:26:27.610
Right?

00:26:27.610 --> 00:26:33.020
So it says, to minimize
some linear function

00:26:33.020 --> 00:26:38.300
over this LMI, this problem,
this is now what-- it's dual,

00:26:38.300 --> 00:26:39.770
and I don't think it's obvious.

00:26:39.770 --> 00:26:42.070
Like, you wouldn't--
I mean, if I--

00:26:42.070 --> 00:26:45.200
it's not difficult
to show parts of it.

00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:48.560
Weak duality is completely
straightforward.

00:26:48.560 --> 00:26:50.870
Strong duality is not obvious.

00:26:50.870 --> 00:26:51.950
But that's it.

00:26:51.950 --> 00:26:53.030
So OK.

00:26:53.030 --> 00:26:54.890
So that covers that.

00:26:54.890 --> 00:26:56.570
And again, you probably
get some chances

00:26:56.570 --> 00:26:59.420
to do some of this on homework.

00:26:59.420 --> 00:27:01.850
OK, actually before
I go on to this,

00:27:01.850 --> 00:27:05.000
I just want to mention
one more topic, which

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:07.490
we didn't cover in
the lecture but we're

00:27:07.490 --> 00:27:09.650
expecting you to know.

00:27:09.650 --> 00:27:13.700
It is actually duality
for feasibility problems.

00:27:13.700 --> 00:27:14.810
That's what it is.

00:27:14.810 --> 00:27:19.370
And the name for that is called
theorems of the alternative.

00:27:19.370 --> 00:27:22.650
The way that works
is pretty simple.

00:27:22.650 --> 00:27:24.830
And I'll just say a
little bit about it,

00:27:24.830 --> 00:27:26.435
but you will encounter it.

00:27:30.120 --> 00:27:31.980
You may-- I think
you're encountering--

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:35.430
you may be encountering it
now on the current homework.

00:27:35.430 --> 00:27:36.900
Is that-- yes?

00:27:36.900 --> 00:27:39.600
Is there a-- is there
an option pricing thing?

00:27:39.600 --> 00:27:40.120
Yes.

00:27:40.120 --> 00:27:40.620
Sorry.

00:27:40.620 --> 00:27:41.610
You will be encountering it now.

00:27:41.610 --> 00:27:42.690
Of course I just gave that away.

00:27:42.690 --> 00:27:43.357
But that's fine.

00:27:43.357 --> 00:27:44.550
Sorry.

00:27:44.550 --> 00:27:46.360
So but that's OK.

00:27:46.360 --> 00:27:46.860
Right?

00:27:46.860 --> 00:27:50.010
So the basic idea is
you write a feasibility

00:27:50.010 --> 00:27:56.550
problem as minimize zero subject
to f i of x is less than 0.

00:27:56.550 --> 00:27:58.050
And let's make it convex.

00:27:58.050 --> 00:28:00.000
So-- it doesn't matter.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:04.750
And h i of x equals 0.

00:28:04.750 --> 00:28:05.250
Right?

00:28:05.250 --> 00:28:07.350
So there's your
feasibility problem.

00:28:07.350 --> 00:28:12.540
This thing has optimal value,
0 if these constraints are

00:28:12.540 --> 00:28:13.500
feasible.

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:16.470
Otherwise, the optimal value
is plus infinity, right?

00:28:16.470 --> 00:28:18.210
Because if it's not
feasible, there's

00:28:18.210 --> 00:28:23.310
no x that satisfies this and by
completely standard assumption,

00:28:23.310 --> 00:28:27.910
or definition, the infimum of
the empty set is plus infinity.

00:28:27.910 --> 00:28:28.410
OK?

00:28:28.410 --> 00:28:33.960
So this has either p star
equals 0 or infinity.

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:36.090
And you can work out
what the dual is.

00:28:36.090 --> 00:28:37.920
It's not complicated at all.

00:28:37.920 --> 00:28:38.430
Right?

00:28:38.430 --> 00:28:39.630
And it's going to turn out.

00:28:39.630 --> 00:28:42.060
You're going to get d star
and then interesting things

00:28:42.060 --> 00:28:42.990
are going to happen.

00:28:42.990 --> 00:28:46.080
This always holds.

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:47.160
That always holds.

00:28:47.160 --> 00:28:49.530
Convex, non-convex, doesn't
make any difference at all.

00:28:49.530 --> 00:28:50.550
It always holds.

00:28:50.550 --> 00:28:51.060
Right?

00:28:51.060 --> 00:28:52.770
And this would tell
you some cool things.

00:28:52.770 --> 00:28:55.560
Like, for example,
if this was-- let's

00:28:55.560 --> 00:28:58.560
say this is a set of
non-convex inequalities.

00:28:58.560 --> 00:29:02.190
Right but if I form
the dual, and the dual,

00:29:02.190 --> 00:29:05.580
if I find the dual is
unbounded above, meaning

00:29:05.580 --> 00:29:08.055
d star is plus infinity,
what can you say?

00:29:12.730 --> 00:29:13.610
It's not feasible.

00:29:13.610 --> 00:29:14.110
Yeah.

00:29:14.110 --> 00:29:15.620
The original one
is not feasible.

00:29:15.620 --> 00:29:17.410
I would say, well, how
could you say that?

00:29:17.410 --> 00:29:19.045
That's-- I'm sorry.

00:29:19.045 --> 00:29:22.450
That problem is like NP hard to
determine if these inequalities

00:29:22.450 --> 00:29:23.770
are feasible.

00:29:23.770 --> 00:29:24.610
Right?

00:29:24.610 --> 00:29:26.990
What would you respond
then when I said that?

00:29:26.990 --> 00:29:27.990
[INAUDIBLE] lower bound.

00:29:27.990 --> 00:29:28.500
Yeah.

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:30.060
You just say, yeah, sorry.

00:29:30.060 --> 00:29:32.910
But it's completely elementary
that this is a lower bound

00:29:32.910 --> 00:29:34.000
on the optimal value.

00:29:34.000 --> 00:29:36.690
And in fact, it
would be good enough

00:29:36.690 --> 00:29:38.100
if I worked out
the dual of this.

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:41.850
If I found a point you
know, some lambda and nus

00:29:41.850 --> 00:29:46.860
for the dual, and I evaluated
it, and d star was equal to 1.

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:50.200
What could you conclude?

00:29:50.200 --> 00:29:51.450
What do you conclude?

00:29:51.450 --> 00:29:52.170
It's infeasible.

00:29:52.170 --> 00:29:53.070
It's infeasible.

00:29:53.070 --> 00:29:54.510
And the argument would
go like this, which

00:29:54.510 --> 00:29:55.718
actually kind of interesting.

00:29:55.718 --> 00:29:56.220
Right?

00:29:56.220 --> 00:29:59.380
That number is either
0 or plus infinity.

00:29:59.380 --> 00:29:59.880
OK?

00:29:59.880 --> 00:30:03.030
If I tell you one is a lower
bound on it, well, hello?

00:30:03.030 --> 00:30:06.880
But now, the only possible
value for this is plus infinity.

00:30:06.880 --> 00:30:10.290
And that means precisely that
your problem is infeasible.

00:30:10.290 --> 00:30:11.980
Everybody got this?

00:30:11.980 --> 00:30:12.480
OK.

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:14.423
So I mean, that was
just a whirlwind tour.

00:30:14.423 --> 00:30:16.590
You'll find-- and these are
generally called theorem

00:30:16.590 --> 00:30:18.780
of the alternatives
because they basically say,

00:30:18.780 --> 00:30:20.880
this set of-- when it's--

00:30:20.880 --> 00:30:24.750
OK, in the convex case, it
says, this set of inequalities

00:30:24.750 --> 00:30:29.160
has a solution, if and only
if, this other set does not.

00:30:29.160 --> 00:30:30.570
Everybody got that?

00:30:30.570 --> 00:30:33.120
I'm just giving
you the big high--

00:30:33.120 --> 00:30:36.480
big, broad strokes
description of the topic.

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:37.140
Everybody?

00:30:37.140 --> 00:30:39.840
And there's some famous ones,
like for linear inequalities,

00:30:39.840 --> 00:30:41.010
it's called Farkas--

00:30:41.010 --> 00:30:42.810
the Farkas' Lemma or
something like that.

00:30:42.810 --> 00:30:44.940
Right?

00:30:44.940 --> 00:30:47.710
And this comes up in
lots of different things.

00:30:47.710 --> 00:30:51.150
One is, it's kind
of the basis of most

00:30:51.150 --> 00:30:54.270
of a lot of classical
economics and finance.

00:30:54.270 --> 00:30:54.930
Right?

00:30:54.930 --> 00:30:57.857
Because there, everything
would be based on the idea--

00:30:57.857 --> 00:30:59.940
I'd say here's a bunch of
stuff you can invest in.

00:30:59.940 --> 00:31:02.010
If you can invest
in these things

00:31:02.010 --> 00:31:04.380
and never lose
money in all cases,

00:31:04.380 --> 00:31:07.600
and sometimes-- and under
some circumstances make money,

00:31:07.600 --> 00:31:09.450
that's called an arbitrage.

00:31:09.450 --> 00:31:09.960
OK?

00:31:09.960 --> 00:31:16.220
And then a lot of finance says--
or a lot of classical finance

00:31:16.220 --> 00:31:18.360
says that can't happen.

00:31:18.360 --> 00:31:18.930
Right?

00:31:18.930 --> 00:31:23.910
Because if that happens, the
prices would readjust in such

00:31:23.910 --> 00:31:26.640
a way that it can't-- somebody
would go in and exploit it.

00:31:26.640 --> 00:31:28.515
And blah, blah, blah,
all that kind of stuff.

00:31:28.515 --> 00:31:30.000
Actually my friends
at hedge funds

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:32.460
will assure you it can happen.

00:31:32.460 --> 00:31:34.530
So yeah.

00:31:34.530 --> 00:31:37.470
Which is why actually I know
some-- one of my friends

00:31:37.470 --> 00:31:39.780
there said, there
was one department

00:31:39.780 --> 00:31:44.010
in a candidate's background
that they would just instantly

00:31:44.010 --> 00:31:46.150
throw it away.

00:31:46.150 --> 00:31:47.430
Economics.

00:31:47.430 --> 00:31:48.160
I was like, why?

00:31:48.160 --> 00:31:50.535
They said, oh, we'll take--
oh, people in EE, we love it.

00:31:50.535 --> 00:31:51.860
Applied physics, oh fantastic.

00:31:51.860 --> 00:31:52.360
Math?

00:31:52.360 --> 00:31:52.990
Great.

00:31:52.990 --> 00:31:54.910
I was like, why not economics?

00:31:54.910 --> 00:31:56.425
And they said, why?

00:31:56.425 --> 00:31:58.300
They looked at me and
they said, why would we

00:31:58.300 --> 00:32:02.470
hire someone who comes
from a field where

00:32:02.470 --> 00:32:05.170
the most deeply held
belief, that where

00:32:05.170 --> 00:32:08.710
the entire thing is constructed
on absence of arbitrage.

00:32:08.710 --> 00:32:10.570
He said, which is
precisely what we do.

00:32:10.570 --> 00:32:12.460
So that kind of-- anyway.

00:32:12.460 --> 00:32:13.210
We all laughed.

00:32:13.210 --> 00:32:15.680
It was mostly a joke,
but not entirely.

00:32:15.680 --> 00:32:17.050
I have to say, sorry.

00:32:17.050 --> 00:32:17.560
OK.

00:32:17.560 --> 00:32:19.450
So OK.

00:32:19.450 --> 00:32:20.260
All right.

00:32:20.260 --> 00:32:23.350
This actually finishes up, like
part one of the class, which

00:32:23.350 --> 00:32:25.360
is all the theory.

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:27.880
I mean, it's already
obvious that it's useful

00:32:27.880 --> 00:32:30.880
and what we're going to do now
for the next like three, four--

00:32:30.880 --> 00:32:35.380
three weeks, I think, is we're
going to look at applications

00:32:35.380 --> 00:32:38.338
broadly grouped.

00:32:38.338 --> 00:32:39.880
And what you'll see
is after a while,

00:32:39.880 --> 00:32:42.005
they start all looking the
same because guess what?

00:32:42.005 --> 00:32:44.170
They are all the same.

00:32:44.170 --> 00:32:45.550
But instead of
doing it that way,

00:32:45.550 --> 00:32:47.980
we'll have some
organization and actually,

00:32:47.980 --> 00:32:49.660
what you should
be figuring out is

00:32:49.660 --> 00:32:52.995
just-- you're going to just get
the idea of things you can do

00:32:52.995 --> 00:32:55.120
and then you'll be doing
practical problems on them

00:32:55.120 --> 00:32:56.270
and all that sort of stuff.

00:32:56.270 --> 00:33:00.550
So we'll start with just
approximation and fitting.

00:33:00.550 --> 00:33:03.850
So we'll start with
some very simple ones,

00:33:03.850 --> 00:33:08.020
norm approximation
says, you know,

00:33:08.020 --> 00:33:11.710
I have a matrix a and b
that's data in the problem.

00:33:11.710 --> 00:33:14.560
And you would like to
choose x to minimize

00:33:14.560 --> 00:33:18.040
the norm of a x minus b.

00:33:18.040 --> 00:33:22.670
I don't know, lots of ways
you can think of this, right?

00:33:22.670 --> 00:33:27.490
So you could say that,
for example x star

00:33:27.490 --> 00:33:30.370
is the point for
which a x star, that's

00:33:30.370 --> 00:33:31.930
the point in the
range of a that's

00:33:31.930 --> 00:33:33.777
closest to b as
measured by this norm.

00:33:33.777 --> 00:33:35.110
I haven't said what the norm is.

00:33:35.110 --> 00:33:36.220
It's any norm, right?

00:33:36.220 --> 00:33:39.970
So that would be that-- so you
could do L1 approximation, L

00:33:39.970 --> 00:33:43.810
infinity approximation, right?

00:33:43.810 --> 00:33:47.355
In estimation, this
would come up this way.

00:33:47.355 --> 00:33:50.940
You would come up
and this is not yet

00:33:50.940 --> 00:33:52.990
with a statistical
interpretation.

00:33:52.990 --> 00:33:56.250
So at first, it's just a
common sense interpretation

00:33:56.250 --> 00:33:57.520
and it would go like this.

00:33:57.520 --> 00:34:00.570
You'd say, I have a
linear measurement model

00:34:00.570 --> 00:34:01.560
and it looks like this.

00:34:01.560 --> 00:34:04.248
X are the parameters
you want to estimate.

00:34:04.248 --> 00:34:05.040
Like, I don't know.

00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:07.530
They could be things
you can't directly

00:34:07.530 --> 00:34:11.820
measure, like I could set off
explosives, have some geophones

00:34:11.820 --> 00:34:14.190
or whatever that
would look at the--

00:34:14.190 --> 00:34:15.420
or I could flay--

00:34:15.420 --> 00:34:19.409
I could fly a magnetic
anomaly detector

00:34:19.409 --> 00:34:23.969
over some region, which would
detect incredibly small changes

00:34:23.969 --> 00:34:26.830
in the magnetic field.

00:34:26.830 --> 00:34:29.130
And this would have
something to do

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:31.889
with what's under-- what's
a kilometer underground?

00:34:31.889 --> 00:34:32.800
Which I don't know.

00:34:32.800 --> 00:34:34.330
And that's what x would be.

00:34:34.330 --> 00:34:37.199
So a, people referred
to as-- actually,

00:34:37.199 --> 00:34:40.110
this very interesting names
for-- one is called the model.

00:34:40.110 --> 00:34:42.000
It's also called
the forward model.

00:34:42.000 --> 00:34:43.739
That's very interesting
name for it.

00:34:43.739 --> 00:34:45.179
Right?

00:34:45.179 --> 00:34:46.690
And anyway, so that's that.

00:34:46.690 --> 00:34:49.210
So the forward model usually
comes from, let's say physics,

00:34:49.210 --> 00:34:50.920
and it tells you, well, yeah.

00:34:50.920 --> 00:34:54.670
If there were these point
sources here, then--

00:34:54.670 --> 00:34:57.490
and they diffused according
to this model in physics,

00:34:57.490 --> 00:34:59.920
then these are
the concentrations

00:34:59.920 --> 00:35:03.070
of carbon dioxide we would
measure at various places.

00:35:03.070 --> 00:35:04.180
Everybody following this?

00:35:04.180 --> 00:35:06.040
I mean this is just
the basic idea of this.

00:35:06.040 --> 00:35:09.190
So that's typically called
the model or the forward model

00:35:09.190 --> 00:35:10.610
or something like that.

00:35:10.610 --> 00:35:12.430
And then, of course,
no one would believe

00:35:12.430 --> 00:35:14.360
y equals a x for many reasons.

00:35:14.360 --> 00:35:14.860
Right?

00:35:14.860 --> 00:35:17.690
Because there's stuff--
at the very least,

00:35:17.690 --> 00:35:19.360
you might have
just sensor error.

00:35:19.360 --> 00:35:21.280
Like how well did you measure--

00:35:21.280 --> 00:35:22.930
how well do you
measure carbon dioxide?

00:35:22.930 --> 00:35:26.182
And you go, it's plus
minus whatever three parts

00:35:26.182 --> 00:35:27.640
per million or
something like that.

00:35:27.640 --> 00:35:28.600
I mean, minimum, right?

00:35:28.600 --> 00:35:30.850
You're going to have that
and it could be other stuff.

00:35:30.850 --> 00:35:32.020
So that's this model.

00:35:32.020 --> 00:35:37.600
And the point here is you
know y, you don't know x

00:35:37.600 --> 00:35:39.790
and you don't know v. And so--

00:35:39.790 --> 00:35:44.150
but someone asks you
to please guess x.

00:35:44.150 --> 00:35:47.120
And so what this does
is actually interesting.

00:35:47.120 --> 00:35:49.955
By the way, because I haven't
said anything about v,

00:35:49.955 --> 00:35:52.020
any possible value of x--

00:35:52.020 --> 00:35:53.780
any value of x is possible.

00:35:53.780 --> 00:35:54.320
Right?

00:35:54.320 --> 00:35:56.930
Because then I would
just change v so to v

00:35:56.930 --> 00:35:59.467
would be y minus a x
which is the residual.

00:35:59.467 --> 00:36:01.550
Actually, some people call
that as beautiful name,

00:36:01.550 --> 00:36:03.453
they call it the
physics residual.

00:36:03.453 --> 00:36:04.370
It's a beautiful name.

00:36:04.370 --> 00:36:06.203
That's when this is
kind of very-- when it's

00:36:06.203 --> 00:36:07.610
a physics real thing, right?

00:36:07.610 --> 00:36:09.500
Or something like
the physics residual.

00:36:09.500 --> 00:36:12.170
And then they-- so you
could explain everything

00:36:12.170 --> 00:36:13.072
by physics residual.

00:36:13.072 --> 00:36:15.530
So I could say, well, I took
my carbon dioxide measurements

00:36:15.530 --> 00:36:16.682
and you go, cool.

00:36:16.682 --> 00:36:18.140
Where do you think
the sources are?

00:36:18.140 --> 00:36:19.310
And you go, oh yeah.

00:36:19.310 --> 00:36:21.390
I think I decided
there aren't any.

00:36:21.390 --> 00:36:23.073
And you go, excuse me?

00:36:23.073 --> 00:36:24.740
We took all these
measurements and we're

00:36:24.740 --> 00:36:27.260
getting all these carbon
dioxide things like what are you

00:36:27.260 --> 00:36:27.660
talking about?

00:36:27.660 --> 00:36:28.452
And go, no, no, no.

00:36:28.452 --> 00:36:30.355
I'm pretty sure it's just zero.

00:36:30.355 --> 00:36:32.480
And you'd say, but what
are all those measurements?

00:36:32.480 --> 00:36:34.460
And you go, oh that's
just measurement error.

00:36:34.460 --> 00:36:35.120
And you're like-- anyway.

00:36:35.120 --> 00:36:36.245
So you see what I'm saying?

00:36:36.245 --> 00:36:38.540
So you can say
anything you like.

00:36:38.540 --> 00:36:42.350
All right so you need
some idea of how--

00:36:42.350 --> 00:36:44.540
because it means you
can say you actually

00:36:44.540 --> 00:36:46.838
kind of like weirdly legal--
although it is weird,

00:36:46.838 --> 00:36:49.130
you could actually announce
that it's anything you like

00:36:49.130 --> 00:36:55.010
and cover it up by presumed
measurement error, right?

00:36:55.010 --> 00:36:59.090
So what you need is you need
to talk about plausibility

00:36:59.090 --> 00:37:02.450
of the value v. Now
later, we'll be doing this

00:37:02.450 --> 00:37:03.920
in a statistical framework.

00:37:03.920 --> 00:37:05.930
V will have a
distribution, and we'll

00:37:05.930 --> 00:37:07.430
look at something
like the maximum--

00:37:07.430 --> 00:37:09.810
we'll look at maximum likelihood
or something like that.

00:37:09.810 --> 00:37:12.200
But for now, we're
just saying, I

00:37:12.200 --> 00:37:14.960
need some measure of
plausibility of v,

00:37:14.960 --> 00:37:18.350
and the measure
here is this norm.

00:37:18.350 --> 00:37:22.220
So you simply say, I don't
know what the measurement error

00:37:22.220 --> 00:37:24.800
is but I-- but my
basic assumption

00:37:24.800 --> 00:37:29.000
is that this norm
expresses implausibility

00:37:29.000 --> 00:37:30.108
for my measurement errors.

00:37:30.108 --> 00:37:30.900
Everybody got that?

00:37:30.900 --> 00:37:33.637
So if it's like L1, that
means something, if it's L2,

00:37:33.637 --> 00:37:34.970
that means something, and so on.

00:37:34.970 --> 00:37:37.880
OK, so and that's this problem.

00:37:37.880 --> 00:37:42.050
OK, another one is
another broad area where

00:37:42.050 --> 00:37:44.100
you get norm is optimal design.

00:37:44.100 --> 00:37:47.550
So x or design variables here.

00:37:47.550 --> 00:37:51.260
A x is a result, and
then x star is the design

00:37:51.260 --> 00:37:53.270
that best approximates
the desired result

00:37:53.270 --> 00:37:55.980
but that's in this
particular norm.

00:37:55.980 --> 00:37:58.850
And so the norm
there says how much

00:37:58.850 --> 00:38:04.340
it irritates you to miss the
target b by a certain amount.

00:38:04.340 --> 00:38:04.910
Right?

00:38:04.910 --> 00:38:10.580
So for example, here
would be, find me

00:38:10.580 --> 00:38:13.430
a bunch of thruster
firings on a satellite.

00:38:13.430 --> 00:38:14.150
That would be x.

00:38:14.150 --> 00:38:16.430
It's a long sequence, right?

00:38:16.430 --> 00:38:18.890
That moves you to
some target place.

00:38:18.890 --> 00:38:20.190
You can't get there.

00:38:20.190 --> 00:38:21.790
And then the question
is, well, if you

00:38:21.790 --> 00:38:22.550
can get there, that's great.

00:38:22.550 --> 00:38:24.230
That's kind of the
solution, right?

00:38:24.230 --> 00:38:26.060
Because norms don't
get smaller than 0.

00:38:26.060 --> 00:38:28.220
But if you can't,
this says, you know

00:38:28.220 --> 00:38:34.340
what would make you
happiest about missing?

00:38:34.340 --> 00:38:36.530
And you'd say, OK,
it'll be the two norm.

00:38:36.530 --> 00:38:38.660
And that just means
get me as close as

00:38:38.660 --> 00:38:41.660
possible to the point that's
moving along your target

00:38:41.660 --> 00:38:42.620
trajectory.

00:38:42.620 --> 00:38:44.750
If you can't get there,
minimize the number

00:38:44.750 --> 00:38:46.410
of meters from that track.

00:38:46.410 --> 00:38:46.910
OK?

00:38:46.910 --> 00:38:48.018
Everybody got that?

00:38:48.018 --> 00:38:50.060
So anyway, it's just to
give you some rough ideas

00:38:50.060 --> 00:38:52.780
of where these come up.

00:38:52.780 --> 00:38:53.350
OK.

00:38:53.350 --> 00:38:57.760
So I mean, least-squares,
everybody here would know.

00:38:57.760 --> 00:39:00.100
So in least-squares, it's a
normal approximation problem

00:39:00.100 --> 00:39:03.940
but then you reflexively,
you square the norm,

00:39:03.940 --> 00:39:06.710
and then you get something
that's differentiable convex.

00:39:06.710 --> 00:39:09.550
You take gradient, you get the
normal equations like this,

00:39:09.550 --> 00:39:11.020
right?

00:39:11.020 --> 00:39:13.100
And then you get some solution.

00:39:13.100 --> 00:39:15.790
So that's quite old.

00:39:15.790 --> 00:39:18.730
When you use infinity norm, it's
called Chebyshev approximation.

00:39:18.730 --> 00:39:20.130
And that's an LP.

00:39:20.130 --> 00:39:23.110
Or I would say, people--
you can convert it to an LP.

00:39:23.110 --> 00:39:24.940
Right?

00:39:24.940 --> 00:39:25.440
Yeah.

00:39:25.440 --> 00:39:28.120
You can have sum of
absolute residuals.

00:39:28.120 --> 00:39:30.550
That's even got a name
in certain fields.

00:39:30.550 --> 00:39:35.230
And again, that's also
can be converted to an LP.

00:39:35.230 --> 00:39:37.360
By the way, people
don't really need

00:39:37.360 --> 00:39:42.040
to know that now because in
things like CVXPY, you just--

00:39:42.040 --> 00:39:43.690
you literally just
type these in.

00:39:43.690 --> 00:39:47.950
You literally-- you write norm,
you know, a x minus b comma 1.

00:39:47.950 --> 00:39:49.600
That's it, right?

00:39:49.600 --> 00:39:51.760
You can just do one line solve.

00:39:51.760 --> 00:39:58.670
So it would be something like
problem of minimize a times

00:39:58.670 --> 00:40:02.245
norm a times x minus b comma 1.

00:40:02.245 --> 00:40:04.370
Then you have a certain
number-- the correct number

00:40:04.370 --> 00:40:07.760
of right parentheses.

00:40:07.760 --> 00:40:09.470
Then you go, dot solve.

00:40:09.470 --> 00:40:11.480
And then I just
constructed a problem

00:40:11.480 --> 00:40:12.800
and solved it in one line.

00:40:12.800 --> 00:40:15.230
Everybody-- hopefully
by now, everybody

00:40:15.230 --> 00:40:16.940
gets what I just said.

00:40:16.940 --> 00:40:19.070
OK?

00:40:19.070 --> 00:40:21.080
so you don't actually
need to know this,

00:40:21.080 --> 00:40:24.600
but it's not bad to know.

00:40:24.600 --> 00:40:25.350
OK.

00:40:25.350 --> 00:40:26.808
Now we're talking
about something--

00:40:26.808 --> 00:40:27.940
this is super interesting.

00:40:27.940 --> 00:40:31.740
It is super important and
don't overinterpret it.

00:40:31.740 --> 00:40:35.560
It is as simple and
dumb as it sounds.

00:40:35.560 --> 00:40:36.060
OK?

00:40:36.060 --> 00:40:40.870
So that's-- and also it
is unbelievably useful.

00:40:40.870 --> 00:40:43.980
So here's what it is.

00:40:43.980 --> 00:40:46.500
I want a x to be equal to b.

00:40:46.500 --> 00:40:48.690
Instead of a norm
though, I'm going

00:40:48.690 --> 00:40:50.080
to have this penalty function.

00:40:50.080 --> 00:40:51.000
And it's going to be separable.

00:40:51.000 --> 00:40:52.470
Separable means
it's a function, is

00:40:52.470 --> 00:40:55.020
a sum of functions of the
individual components that's

00:40:55.020 --> 00:40:55.870
separable.

00:40:55.870 --> 00:40:56.820
So here it is.

00:40:56.820 --> 00:40:59.640
And then these are just
functions from r to r.

00:40:59.640 --> 00:41:03.330
And if it's quadratic,
this becomes least squares.

00:41:03.330 --> 00:41:06.120
If phi of u is the
absolute value of u,

00:41:06.120 --> 00:41:07.890
this is L1 approximation.

00:41:07.890 --> 00:41:10.530
But now we can do
cool things like,

00:41:10.530 --> 00:41:13.080
let's see, this is here's
quadratic, right here,

00:41:13.080 --> 00:41:15.250
this one.

00:41:15.250 --> 00:41:17.310
Here's a crazy one,
deadzone linear.

00:41:17.310 --> 00:41:20.790
So these are just-- these
are not standard names,

00:41:20.790 --> 00:41:21.990
but anyone would get it.

00:41:21.990 --> 00:41:25.900
Deadzone means
there is a zone here

00:41:25.900 --> 00:41:29.050
where you just-- don't care.

00:41:29.050 --> 00:41:32.530
And then outside that zone,
the penalty grows linearly.

00:41:32.530 --> 00:41:34.870
Right?

00:41:34.870 --> 00:41:36.760
And here's another
one, log barrier.

00:41:36.760 --> 00:41:38.560
That's this function here.

00:41:38.560 --> 00:41:44.170
It agrees really closely with
a quadratic for small values,

00:41:44.170 --> 00:41:46.492
but for large values, it
actually-- it starts going up

00:41:46.492 --> 00:41:47.950
and it goes all
the way to infinity

00:41:47.950 --> 00:41:53.630
and outside some band plus minus
a, it's just plus infinity.

00:41:53.630 --> 00:41:54.130
OK?

00:41:54.130 --> 00:41:57.460
So one way-- the way I
actually think about these

00:41:57.460 --> 00:41:59.260
is you anthropomorphize
all this.

00:41:59.260 --> 00:42:01.240
And it's actually
kind of correct.

00:42:01.240 --> 00:42:04.780
So what phi is
telling you is phi

00:42:04.780 --> 00:42:08.500
of r tells you how
irritated you are

00:42:08.500 --> 00:42:12.030
for a residual of that size.

00:42:12.030 --> 00:42:13.770
It's really that
simple and dumb.

00:42:13.770 --> 00:42:15.640
And so for example,
if someone says,

00:42:15.640 --> 00:42:18.030
I'm doing this approximation
with this penalty function,

00:42:18.030 --> 00:42:22.090
what it means is, for
some reason, you don't--

00:42:22.090 --> 00:42:25.470
errors that are
between plus minus a,

00:42:25.470 --> 00:42:28.440
how much do you care about them?

00:42:28.440 --> 00:42:29.370
None.

00:42:29.370 --> 00:42:30.150
Zero.

00:42:30.150 --> 00:42:31.320
You couldn't care less.

00:42:31.320 --> 00:42:32.260
Right?

00:42:32.260 --> 00:42:34.260
Somebody want to give me
a case where that would

00:42:34.260 --> 00:42:37.740
be completely justifiable?

00:42:37.740 --> 00:42:40.230
I mean with a, I guess
a small a or something.

00:42:42.900 --> 00:42:43.830
Yeah.

00:42:43.830 --> 00:42:48.850
[INAUDIBLE] for delivery,
this is [INAUDIBLE]..

00:42:48.850 --> 00:42:50.370
You're trying to
optimize for what?

00:42:50.370 --> 00:42:53.850
Minimum delivery time,
for example, DoorDash.

00:42:53.850 --> 00:42:56.130
Plus minus 1 is the metric.

00:42:56.130 --> 00:42:57.220
OK.

00:42:57.220 --> 00:42:58.200
Yeah.

00:42:58.200 --> 00:43:00.265
Let me-- I didn't quite get it.

00:43:00.265 --> 00:43:01.890
So I mean, here's a
very simple example

00:43:01.890 --> 00:43:06.270
is you measure something, and
the measurements are basically

00:43:06.270 --> 00:43:08.520
done with a 5-bit converter.

00:43:08.520 --> 00:43:11.130
And so what you get
is only-- there's

00:43:11.130 --> 00:43:12.730
only 32 possible values.

00:43:12.730 --> 00:43:15.430
So when you get a measurement,
here's what you don't-- what

00:43:15.430 --> 00:43:15.930
you get--

00:43:15.930 --> 00:43:16.320
I mean, sure.

00:43:16.320 --> 00:43:17.460
I tell you the number in there.

00:43:17.460 --> 00:43:18.700
I pretend that's the number.

00:43:18.700 --> 00:43:21.930
But in fact, any measurement
in this little interval

00:43:21.930 --> 00:43:23.320
would have mapped to that.

00:43:23.320 --> 00:43:25.560
So what that says here
is for the penalty.

00:43:25.560 --> 00:43:29.340
If I represent that
here, someone says, why?

00:43:29.340 --> 00:43:33.960
And you go, hello, it's
a 6-bit a to d converter.

00:43:33.960 --> 00:43:38.310
Like worrying-- anything, if
I interpret any number smaller

00:43:38.310 --> 00:43:40.770
than that, or here,
I'll go into dialect,

00:43:40.770 --> 00:43:42.802
smaller than my LSB
of my converter,

00:43:42.802 --> 00:43:44.260
that was the Least
Significant Bit.

00:43:44.260 --> 00:43:48.110
Then you say, it's completely
fictitious and means nothing.

00:43:48.110 --> 00:43:48.620
Right?

00:43:48.620 --> 00:43:49.880
So that would be that.

00:43:49.880 --> 00:43:50.726
OK.

00:43:50.726 --> 00:43:51.400
[INAUDIBLE]

00:43:51.400 --> 00:43:52.060
Yes?

00:43:52.060 --> 00:43:55.500
[INAUDIBLE]

00:43:55.500 --> 00:43:56.550
Up here?

00:43:56.550 --> 00:43:57.080
Yeah.

00:43:57.080 --> 00:43:58.220
Yeah.

00:43:58.220 --> 00:44:01.470
Actually, there is here, yeah.

00:44:01.470 --> 00:44:03.750
Because I want to
measure the residuals.

00:44:03.750 --> 00:44:06.420
We could do the same thing with
an inequality constraint here.

00:44:06.420 --> 00:44:08.070
If you were--

00:44:08.070 --> 00:44:10.240
I mean, it's a bit stranger,
but you could do that.

00:44:10.240 --> 00:44:11.970
You could certainly do it
with an inequality constraint

00:44:11.970 --> 00:44:12.450
as well.

00:44:12.450 --> 00:44:12.950
Right?

00:44:12.950 --> 00:44:15.450
If you'd say I'd like
these inequalities to hold,

00:44:15.450 --> 00:44:18.240
and then you'd say,
yeah but I have weird--

00:44:18.240 --> 00:44:22.140
I have weird personal opinions
about how much it irritates

00:44:22.140 --> 00:44:24.450
me to violate
inequalities, then it

00:44:24.450 --> 00:44:26.100
would be exactly an inequality.

00:44:26.100 --> 00:44:28.830
And everything
would work the same.

00:44:28.830 --> 00:44:31.030
OK.

00:44:31.030 --> 00:44:33.460
So we'll just look
at a little example.

00:44:33.460 --> 00:44:37.240
Here is it just like,
this is a matrix which

00:44:37.240 --> 00:44:38.680
is 100 high, 30 wide.

00:44:38.680 --> 00:44:44.020
So you have 30 x's and 100
basically equations, right?

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:45.430
Generate completely randomly.

00:44:45.430 --> 00:44:46.450
So this is just--

00:44:46.450 --> 00:44:49.340
but this will tell you a lot.

00:44:49.340 --> 00:44:51.530
So let's start with
least-squares, that's here.

00:44:51.530 --> 00:44:54.310
So if you do least-squares, and
then you look at the history--

00:44:54.310 --> 00:44:58.720
then you look at the residual,
the distribution of residuals,

00:44:58.720 --> 00:45:00.940
then you see
something reasonable.

00:45:00.940 --> 00:45:02.250
This shows you the penalty.

00:45:02.250 --> 00:45:06.310
This shows you how much
it irritated you to have

00:45:06.310 --> 00:45:07.580
a residual at that level.

00:45:07.580 --> 00:45:11.260
And what you can see is, this
is obvious for a quadratic.

00:45:11.260 --> 00:45:12.680
Around in here,
it's really small.

00:45:12.680 --> 00:45:13.180
Why?

00:45:13.180 --> 00:45:17.650
Because well, for a
quadratic, if an error,

00:45:17.650 --> 00:45:19.630
if a residual is
small, the quadratic

00:45:19.630 --> 00:45:24.360
charges you small squared, and
small squared is very small.

00:45:24.360 --> 00:45:26.900
In fact, some people
even use the language

00:45:26.900 --> 00:45:30.080
where very small means--

00:45:30.080 --> 00:45:31.940
actually literally
means small squared.

00:45:31.940 --> 00:45:35.427
Very, very small means
small cubed, and whatever.

00:45:35.427 --> 00:45:36.260
Something like that.

00:45:36.260 --> 00:45:39.002
And incredibly small
indeed means something

00:45:39.002 --> 00:45:40.460
like the fourth
power or something.

00:45:40.460 --> 00:45:42.290
That would be the
analog of like jerk

00:45:42.290 --> 00:45:45.065
and what's one up from jerk?

00:45:45.750 --> 00:45:46.250
Snap.

00:45:46.250 --> 00:45:46.790
What is that?

00:45:46.790 --> 00:45:47.180
Snap.

00:45:47.180 --> 00:45:47.660
There you go.

00:45:47.660 --> 00:45:48.200
OK, fine.

00:45:48.200 --> 00:45:49.190
So OK.

00:45:49.190 --> 00:45:50.810
And you can see what happens.

00:45:50.810 --> 00:45:53.370
And you can even go
back and say, like, ooh,

00:45:53.370 --> 00:45:55.010
you had some big residuals here.

00:45:55.010 --> 00:45:56.943
Right?

00:45:56.943 --> 00:45:58.610
In fact, we can even
anthropomorphize it

00:45:58.610 --> 00:46:00.620
and say, like ooh,
that one really

00:46:00.620 --> 00:46:02.540
hurt because look at
the height of this thing

00:46:02.540 --> 00:46:05.150
compared to over here.

00:46:05.150 --> 00:46:08.420
OK, so this is least-squares
traditional thing.

00:46:08.420 --> 00:46:12.500
For fun, let's see-- oh, let's
look at log barrier next.

00:46:12.500 --> 00:46:16.580
For log barrier,
this thing actually

00:46:16.580 --> 00:46:19.820
matches that almost exactly
for small residuals.

00:46:19.820 --> 00:46:21.890
But what happens
with log barrier is

00:46:21.890 --> 00:46:25.340
as your residuals get bigger
and bigger, unlikely squares,

00:46:25.340 --> 00:46:26.360
you start getting--

00:46:26.360 --> 00:46:30.830
you get more and more upset
compared to least squares

00:46:30.830 --> 00:46:32.600
because the log
barrier is bigger

00:46:32.600 --> 00:46:34.640
than the square function, right?

00:46:34.640 --> 00:46:37.940
And so what happens is you
can see it-- by the way,

00:46:37.940 --> 00:46:39.948
all residuals were
between plus and minus 1.

00:46:39.948 --> 00:46:40.490
It had to be.

00:46:40.490 --> 00:46:41.510
Otherwise you'd get infinity.

00:46:41.510 --> 00:46:43.310
Actually the problem would
be infeasible at that point,

00:46:43.310 --> 00:46:43.700
right?

00:46:43.700 --> 00:46:45.908
But the point is they're
all between there and there,

00:46:45.908 --> 00:46:47.910
and you can almost
say something like,

00:46:47.910 --> 00:46:50.420
well this is least-squares,
but I put a big old--

00:46:50.420 --> 00:46:52.730
I put a clamp on it
and tighten the clamp

00:46:52.730 --> 00:46:55.370
so that all the residuals have
to be between plus and minus 1.

00:46:55.370 --> 00:46:57.620
Everybody-- I'm just--

00:46:57.620 --> 00:46:59.480
but there's nothing deep here.

00:46:59.480 --> 00:47:02.420
So don't overinterpret.

00:47:02.420 --> 00:47:04.167
Deadzone linear is really cool.

00:47:04.167 --> 00:47:05.250
Let's take a look at that.

00:47:05.250 --> 00:47:11.240
So deadzone linear is kind
of interesting because--

00:47:11.240 --> 00:47:13.010
first of all, what
you did is, you

00:47:13.010 --> 00:47:16.850
told the, let's say the
solver or whatever, that once

00:47:16.850 --> 00:47:19.670
a residual is between plus
and minus a half, yeah,

00:47:19.670 --> 00:47:23.270
plus and minus a half, you
couldn't care less at all.

00:47:23.270 --> 00:47:27.540
If you're outside, you
care, but in fact, it's

00:47:27.540 --> 00:47:28.560
going up linearly.

00:47:28.560 --> 00:47:31.360
And we'll talk about
that in a minute.

00:47:31.360 --> 00:47:35.730
And so, not surprisingly,
look at the residuals you get.

00:47:35.730 --> 00:47:38.970
And somebody give me
some English to explain--

00:47:38.970 --> 00:47:42.270
to anthropomorphize
this and say, why do you

00:47:42.270 --> 00:47:44.190
get a whole bunch of
things shoved right up

00:47:44.190 --> 00:47:45.480
against these two boundaries?

00:47:45.480 --> 00:47:47.455
Somebody want to-- this?

00:47:47.455 --> 00:47:48.580
I mean, you can't be wrong.

00:47:48.580 --> 00:47:51.220
This is just-- we're just
talking and anthropomorphizing

00:47:51.220 --> 00:47:51.720
it, right?

00:47:51.720 --> 00:47:54.275
So what would you say?

00:47:54.275 --> 00:47:56.450
[INAUDIBLE] optomize it.

00:47:56.450 --> 00:47:57.800
That there's no benefit to me.

00:47:57.800 --> 00:47:58.010
Yeah.

00:47:58.010 --> 00:47:58.430
That's right.

00:47:58.430 --> 00:47:59.847
You'd say, well,
you know, what it

00:47:59.847 --> 00:48:03.020
did was it shoved a
whole bunch of residuals

00:48:03.020 --> 00:48:05.750
down to the boundary of
where you don't care,

00:48:05.750 --> 00:48:08.420
and why should it expend any
effort to make it smaller?

00:48:08.420 --> 00:48:09.440
[INAUDIBLE]

00:48:09.440 --> 00:48:10.220
Perfect.

00:48:10.220 --> 00:48:10.790
Perfect.

00:48:10.790 --> 00:48:11.670
Everybody got that?

00:48:11.670 --> 00:48:12.170
Right.

00:48:12.170 --> 00:48:14.990
So OK, it's kind of cool.

00:48:14.990 --> 00:48:16.700
Oh, and if I'd say,
hey, look at this.

00:48:16.700 --> 00:48:17.990
You've got a bigger--

00:48:17.990 --> 00:48:21.260
you have a couple of bigger
residuals than the square.

00:48:21.260 --> 00:48:23.240
I mean, this is hard
to see on the scale,

00:48:23.240 --> 00:48:24.657
but we'll get to
that in a minute.

00:48:24.657 --> 00:48:25.790
Let's go to the L1 one.

00:48:25.790 --> 00:48:28.220
This is L1.

00:48:28.220 --> 00:48:30.080
Anybody notice
anything on the L1?

00:48:30.080 --> 00:48:32.000
I mean, this is kind
of an obvious thing.

00:48:32.000 --> 00:48:35.930
A whole bunch of the-- a whole
bunch of the residuals are 0.

00:48:35.930 --> 00:48:39.320
Not a whole bunch, like 35 or
something 30, or something.

00:48:39.320 --> 00:48:43.010
Everybody got it?

00:48:43.010 --> 00:48:46.110
To anthropomorphize that,
we would go like this.

00:48:46.110 --> 00:48:48.620
Let's consider the square
function, the square penalty,

00:48:48.620 --> 00:48:52.490
and the absolute value penalty
for small residuals, right?

00:48:52.490 --> 00:48:55.820
When you have a square penalty,
and a residual becomes small,

00:48:55.820 --> 00:48:57.890
you start caring
about it a lot less

00:48:57.890 --> 00:49:01.850
because the amount you care
about it, is small squared.

00:49:01.850 --> 00:49:03.810
Everybody got it?

00:49:03.810 --> 00:49:04.680
OK.

00:49:04.680 --> 00:49:08.220
For an absolute
value, what happens

00:49:08.220 --> 00:49:11.850
is the marginal
amount by which you

00:49:11.850 --> 00:49:17.500
care about reducing a residual
keeps up until you hit 0.

00:49:17.500 --> 00:49:18.000
OK?

00:49:18.000 --> 00:49:20.130
So in least-squares,
the difference

00:49:20.130 --> 00:49:26.640
between having a residual of
9 and a residual of 10 is big.

00:49:26.640 --> 00:49:28.200
And the residual--
the difference

00:49:28.200 --> 00:49:31.140
between having a residual
of 1 and 2 is a lot less.

00:49:31.140 --> 00:49:32.070
Everybody got it?

00:49:32.070 --> 00:49:34.403
You can just square those
numbers and you figure it out.

00:49:34.403 --> 00:49:35.480
Right?

00:49:35.480 --> 00:49:38.430
For an absolute
value, the difference

00:49:38.430 --> 00:49:41.070
between having a residual
of 9 and a residual of 10

00:49:41.070 --> 00:49:43.560
is identical to the
difference between having

00:49:43.560 --> 00:49:45.000
a residual of 1 and 2.

00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:46.658
Everybody got that?

00:49:46.658 --> 00:49:47.700
This is completely just--

00:49:47.700 --> 00:49:49.470
I'm just saying that--

00:49:49.470 --> 00:49:51.150
So that's actually
one interesting thing

00:49:51.150 --> 00:49:52.650
you'll see about a
penalty function.

00:49:52.650 --> 00:49:57.030
If a penalty function
has a sharp point,

00:49:57.030 --> 00:50:01.190
then you're actually--
you will expect, actually,

00:50:01.190 --> 00:50:03.980
the solution to
have lots of zeros.

00:50:03.980 --> 00:50:05.360
And someone would say, why?

00:50:05.360 --> 00:50:07.130
There's lots of ways
to see this, right?

00:50:07.130 --> 00:50:09.560
And in other contexts,
like in compressed sensing,

00:50:09.560 --> 00:50:11.270
and all this kind
of stuff, people

00:50:11.270 --> 00:50:13.550
can construct incredibly
complicated theories

00:50:13.550 --> 00:50:14.130
about this.

00:50:14.130 --> 00:50:16.070
But the point is,
it's just because

00:50:16.070 --> 00:50:20.630
the marginal improvement of
making a residual smaller,

00:50:20.630 --> 00:50:22.850
if we anthropomorphize
the whole thing,

00:50:22.850 --> 00:50:26.360
basically keeps up until it's 0.

00:50:26.360 --> 00:50:27.200
OK?

00:50:27.200 --> 00:50:30.847
By the way, the opposite is
like a square is kind of chill.

00:50:30.847 --> 00:50:32.930
It's like, once it gets
small, it's small squared.

00:50:32.930 --> 00:50:34.597
You still care, but
you care a lot less.

00:50:34.597 --> 00:50:35.450
Everybody got that?

00:50:35.450 --> 00:50:37.820
And then deadzone
linear, extreme?

00:50:37.820 --> 00:50:39.140
It's like, nope.

00:50:39.140 --> 00:50:41.930
Actually, once you get in
this band, couldn't care less.

00:50:41.930 --> 00:50:42.830
Everybody got that?

00:50:42.830 --> 00:50:44.570
So that's what a
penalty-- that's

00:50:44.570 --> 00:50:48.710
what the qualities of a
penalty function near 0 matter.

00:50:48.710 --> 00:50:49.790
Right?

00:50:49.790 --> 00:50:53.090
At the other end we look at the
qualities of a penalty function

00:50:53.090 --> 00:50:55.880
for large things because
that tells you again

00:50:55.880 --> 00:50:57.480
to anthropomorphize this.

00:50:57.480 --> 00:50:59.250
How-- well, OK.

00:50:59.250 --> 00:51:00.240
This is not it.

00:51:00.240 --> 00:51:03.450
I was anthropomorphizing the
solution method but here.

00:51:03.450 --> 00:51:05.560
What that tells you is this.

00:51:05.560 --> 00:51:09.510
When things grow
linearly, that says

00:51:09.510 --> 00:51:14.680
you're more chill about having
a couple of big residuals.

00:51:14.680 --> 00:51:15.450
I mean, right?

00:51:15.450 --> 00:51:18.930
Because if I had a residual of
like 10, and I'm doing square,

00:51:18.930 --> 00:51:21.850
that's like, it's a big thing.

00:51:21.850 --> 00:51:22.350
OK?

00:51:22.350 --> 00:51:25.650
If you have a residual that--

00:51:25.650 --> 00:51:28.170
but for an absolute value, that
doesn't bother you as much.

00:51:28.170 --> 00:51:29.310
Everybody got this?

00:51:29.310 --> 00:51:31.080
Right?

00:51:31.080 --> 00:51:33.123
OK, so that's going
to shape-- and that's

00:51:33.123 --> 00:51:34.290
going to come up as a theme.

00:51:34.290 --> 00:51:35.957
We'll see it's going
to end up with what

00:51:35.957 --> 00:51:38.040
so-called robust
estimators that we'll see.

00:51:38.040 --> 00:51:39.060
Yeah?

00:51:39.060 --> 00:51:42.870
Shouldn't the [INAUDIBLE]
for b equals 2 and log values

00:51:42.870 --> 00:51:45.660
still be a little more
concentrated around 0

00:51:45.660 --> 00:51:48.480
because there's still
some increasing penalty

00:51:48.480 --> 00:51:49.950
as you go away from 0, right?

00:51:49.950 --> 00:51:51.300
Yeah.

00:51:51.300 --> 00:51:52.080
[INAUDIBLE]

00:51:52.080 --> 00:51:52.580
Yeah.

00:51:52.580 --> 00:51:56.610
So the question is why didn't
these concentrate closer?

00:51:56.610 --> 00:51:58.340
Yeah.

00:51:58.340 --> 00:52:00.120
It's an interesting question.

00:52:00.120 --> 00:52:02.390
I can assure you that
these are the solutions.

00:52:02.390 --> 00:52:03.440
Right?

00:52:03.440 --> 00:52:06.650
And this is literally
randomly chosen data.

00:52:06.650 --> 00:52:09.590
Actually, what it tells you
is this is the best it can do.

00:52:09.590 --> 00:52:12.290
That would be my
best answer to that.

00:52:12.290 --> 00:52:14.450
But certainly, I
can tell you this.

00:52:14.450 --> 00:52:17.030
If it could have concentrated
them closer together,

00:52:17.030 --> 00:52:17.870
it would have.

00:52:17.870 --> 00:52:20.440
How about that?

00:52:20.440 --> 00:52:20.980
Yeah.

00:52:20.980 --> 00:52:22.210
It's a good question.

00:52:22.210 --> 00:52:23.260
OK.

00:52:23.260 --> 00:52:25.990
So let me ask you a couple
of questions just for--

00:52:25.990 --> 00:52:28.480
and then you tell me
what would happen.

00:52:28.480 --> 00:52:32.080
If I told you that my
penalty function is this.

00:52:32.080 --> 00:52:33.250
Here's my penalty function.

00:52:37.480 --> 00:52:38.590
There it is.

00:52:38.590 --> 00:52:39.790
Everybody got it?

00:52:39.790 --> 00:52:42.880
And I want you to tell me
in words what it means.

00:52:42.880 --> 00:52:44.350
What do you care about?

00:52:44.350 --> 00:52:47.290
What bothers you
about residuals?

00:52:47.290 --> 00:52:50.150
So somebody explain it.

00:52:50.150 --> 00:52:52.460
What does it mean?

00:52:52.460 --> 00:52:53.750
Yeah, go ahead.

00:52:53.750 --> 00:52:56.060
You're biased towards
underestimating.

00:52:56.060 --> 00:52:56.940
Yeah, exactly.

00:52:56.940 --> 00:52:57.440
Right.

00:52:57.440 --> 00:53:03.260
So what this says is
that this says that you--

00:53:03.260 --> 00:53:03.980
yeah.

00:53:03.980 --> 00:53:05.840
That it's going to irritate you.

00:53:05.840 --> 00:53:07.587
Positive residuals irritate you.

00:53:07.587 --> 00:53:09.170
I don't know, I'm
going to eyeball it,

00:53:09.170 --> 00:53:12.410
four times more than
negative residuals.

00:53:12.410 --> 00:53:13.040
Right?

00:53:13.040 --> 00:53:14.900
So by the way, you
won't be surprised.

00:53:14.900 --> 00:53:16.942
This function is going to
make an appearance when

00:53:16.942 --> 00:53:18.110
you do quantile estimation.

00:53:18.110 --> 00:53:19.880
But nevertheless--
you might some of you

00:53:19.880 --> 00:53:21.650
maybe already know
that but that's it.

00:53:21.650 --> 00:53:22.430
OK.

00:53:22.430 --> 00:53:23.850
Cool?

00:53:23.850 --> 00:53:25.100
Let's go the other way around.

00:53:25.100 --> 00:53:28.370
What have I told you
that, look, residuals

00:53:28.370 --> 00:53:32.370
between plus and minus a
half, I couldn't care less.

00:53:32.370 --> 00:53:34.910
It's like, our measurements
are not even that good.

00:53:34.910 --> 00:53:36.020
That's fine.

00:53:36.020 --> 00:53:38.660
And then you say, do you have
a preference between being less

00:53:38.660 --> 00:53:41.570
than-- minus a half and more
than a half, and they go, yeah.

00:53:41.570 --> 00:53:44.870
I really don't want my residuals
to be more than a half.

00:53:44.870 --> 00:53:48.085
Somebody suggest a
penalty function.

00:53:51.490 --> 00:53:51.990
Go ahead.

00:53:51.990 --> 00:53:56.750
Suggest a penalty
function for that.

00:53:56.750 --> 00:53:58.430
Same thing, but with a deadzone.

00:53:58.430 --> 00:54:02.570
Yeah, so we say, it's
just flat 0 in here.

00:54:02.570 --> 00:54:06.410
And then this grows steeply
and this grows like that.

00:54:06.410 --> 00:54:07.250
There you go.

00:54:07.250 --> 00:54:08.740
OK?

00:54:08.740 --> 00:54:11.240
All right, and that's-- so, if
you encounter a problem where

00:54:11.240 --> 00:54:14.630
that was your preference,
now you know what to do.

00:54:14.630 --> 00:54:16.340
You just-- and
you just literally

00:54:16.340 --> 00:54:19.310
you type that in, that's two
lines, I could make it one.

00:54:19.310 --> 00:54:21.020
But the point is,
it doesn't matter.

00:54:21.020 --> 00:54:23.360
Everybody got this?

00:54:23.360 --> 00:54:27.540
And what if you
said, you know what?

00:54:27.540 --> 00:54:30.330
Suppose I got even-- suppose
I said, you know what?

00:54:30.330 --> 00:54:33.540
It's OK, I care less if
the residuals are less

00:54:33.540 --> 00:54:35.400
than minus a half,
but actually, when

00:54:35.400 --> 00:54:37.560
they start getting
really negative,

00:54:37.560 --> 00:54:39.330
that irritates me more.

00:54:39.330 --> 00:54:40.650
That irritates me more.

00:54:40.650 --> 00:54:43.440
What would you do then?

00:54:43.440 --> 00:54:46.205
Yeah, you just put some
curvature on this guy,

00:54:46.205 --> 00:54:47.580
and just have it
go up like that.

00:54:47.580 --> 00:54:47.790
Right?

00:54:47.790 --> 00:54:50.070
So I mean, at some point,
these are getting weird.

00:54:50.070 --> 00:54:54.040
But anyway, so
everybody got this?

00:54:54.040 --> 00:54:54.600
Yeah.

00:54:54.600 --> 00:54:56.770
Is it possible to have
a piecewise residual?

00:54:56.770 --> 00:54:59.800
You can absolutely
have a piecewise--

00:54:59.800 --> 00:55:02.530
you can have a piecewise
linear penalty function.

00:55:02.530 --> 00:55:04.420
Absolutely.

00:55:04.420 --> 00:55:07.210
That's not at all uncommon.

00:55:07.210 --> 00:55:07.720
Right?

00:55:07.720 --> 00:55:10.520
So actually, that's a very
common model of fuel usage.

00:55:10.520 --> 00:55:12.005
So it comes up right there.

00:55:12.005 --> 00:55:12.505
Right?

00:55:16.040 --> 00:55:16.540
OK.

00:55:16.540 --> 00:55:18.800
This brings us to
an interesting one.

00:55:18.800 --> 00:55:21.070
It's something everyone
should know about

00:55:21.070 --> 00:55:23.170
because this is, like--

00:55:23.170 --> 00:55:26.050
I would even put something
like this in the top 10

00:55:26.050 --> 00:55:29.500
for the things you should walk
away from this class from.

00:55:29.500 --> 00:55:35.290
Is the so-called Huber penalty.

00:55:35.290 --> 00:55:36.400
Here it is.

00:55:36.400 --> 00:55:40.880
It's quadratic up
to a threshold, m.

00:55:40.880 --> 00:55:42.800
Above that, it grows linearly.

00:55:42.800 --> 00:55:48.980
And so what happens is here's
Huber, and right here at 1,

00:55:48.980 --> 00:55:50.330
it transitions to linear.

00:55:50.330 --> 00:55:52.010
So it's got linear tails here.

00:55:52.010 --> 00:55:54.230
Now the quadratic would
keep going up here,

00:55:54.230 --> 00:55:56.390
and it would start getting
really-- like at 1.5,

00:55:56.390 --> 00:55:57.350
it'd be 2.25.

00:55:57.350 --> 00:55:59.510
It'd be up here, right?

00:55:59.510 --> 00:56:00.290
So quadratic.

00:56:00.290 --> 00:56:03.030
And then what does it mean?

00:56:03.030 --> 00:56:06.420
So somebody-- you know,
anthropomorphize this.

00:56:06.420 --> 00:56:07.920
If someone says,
what are you doing?

00:56:07.920 --> 00:56:09.360
You say, I'm doing a regression.

00:56:09.360 --> 00:56:11.040
What penalty are you using?

00:56:11.040 --> 00:56:12.490
Huber.

00:56:12.490 --> 00:56:15.460
Somebody explain what
it means about how

00:56:15.460 --> 00:56:19.220
you feel about residuals
in your regression model?

00:56:19.220 --> 00:56:20.000
What does it mean?

00:56:22.580 --> 00:56:23.960
Like just in words.

00:56:23.960 --> 00:56:26.610
I don't want no math.

00:56:26.610 --> 00:56:29.050
What would you say?

00:56:29.050 --> 00:56:31.840
That [INAUDIBLE]
residuals and as it

00:56:31.840 --> 00:56:33.635
gets bigger, your [INAUDIBLE].

00:56:33.635 --> 00:56:36.970
Yeah, you'd say, look, I
want to do least-squares,

00:56:36.970 --> 00:56:38.470
I kind of want to
do least-squares.

00:56:38.470 --> 00:56:39.580
But you know what?

00:56:39.580 --> 00:56:42.160
If there has to
be a big residual,

00:56:42.160 --> 00:56:47.090
I'm way more chill than if
I was doing least squares.

00:56:47.090 --> 00:56:49.870
And so, this is
kind of-- so these--

00:56:49.870 --> 00:56:53.968
the fact that these tails here
grow linearly, means-- in fact,

00:56:53.968 --> 00:56:55.510
in statistics, it's
called a robust--

00:56:55.510 --> 00:56:56.660
it's robust penalty.

00:56:56.660 --> 00:56:57.160
Right?

00:56:57.160 --> 00:56:58.750
So it's a robust estimator.

00:56:58.750 --> 00:57:00.250
OK, everybody--

00:57:00.250 --> 00:57:01.630
OK, so it's very innocent.

00:57:01.630 --> 00:57:05.590
It's just-- it works,
you know, I guess,

00:57:05.590 --> 00:57:06.950
we'll look at an example.

00:57:06.950 --> 00:57:09.910
And this is a baby example
just to understand it.

00:57:09.910 --> 00:57:13.600
But so here, we're
literally doing regression

00:57:13.600 --> 00:57:14.440
from high school.

00:57:14.440 --> 00:57:16.090
So here's a bunch
of points here.

00:57:16.090 --> 00:57:17.980
What you might not
see as a point there,

00:57:17.980 --> 00:57:19.810
and there's a point there.

00:57:19.810 --> 00:57:21.790
OK?

00:57:21.790 --> 00:57:27.160
And if you do the least-squares
fit, you get this dashed line.

00:57:27.160 --> 00:57:27.700
OK?

00:57:27.700 --> 00:57:31.070
And actually somebody
explained that to me.

00:57:31.070 --> 00:57:36.500
Why would that be the regression
fit to those 42 points?

00:57:36.500 --> 00:57:39.900
These 40 points in these
two weird ones out here?

00:57:39.900 --> 00:57:41.417
Like what happened?

00:57:41.417 --> 00:57:43.750
The squared residual is very
large for those two points.

00:57:43.750 --> 00:57:44.940
So it tries to minimize those.

00:57:44.940 --> 00:57:45.440
Right.

00:57:45.440 --> 00:57:48.360
So what it did was
for these two points,

00:57:48.360 --> 00:57:51.780
the squared residual
was way big, and that--

00:57:51.780 --> 00:57:53.280
we can anthropomorphize
it and said

00:57:53.280 --> 00:57:56.730
that that put very
strong incentive

00:57:56.730 --> 00:58:02.140
to change the fit to reduce
those two gigantic residuals.

00:58:02.140 --> 00:58:02.640
OK?

00:58:02.640 --> 00:58:04.980
And the result is it
got torqued this way.

00:58:04.980 --> 00:58:06.990
Actually, we can give
a beautiful mechanical

00:58:06.990 --> 00:58:08.670
interpretation.

00:58:08.670 --> 00:58:11.545
So again, if you don't
mind, if you don't--

00:58:11.545 --> 00:58:13.920
just anyone can know this kind
of mechanical engineering.

00:58:13.920 --> 00:58:15.460
Very simple.

00:58:15.460 --> 00:58:17.760
What you do is you
make your penalty

00:58:17.760 --> 00:58:20.130
the potential of a spring.

00:58:20.130 --> 00:58:23.070
So a quadratic is what's
called a Hooke spring.

00:58:23.070 --> 00:58:26.700
So you pull it out,
some amount of distance,

00:58:26.700 --> 00:58:28.427
actually, it's a
symmetric spring,

00:58:28.427 --> 00:58:30.510
you can pull it to the
left, pull it to the right.

00:58:30.510 --> 00:58:32.190
And the potential
energy is going

00:58:32.190 --> 00:58:34.410
to be the square of
the displacement.

00:58:34.410 --> 00:58:35.430
Everybody got that?

00:58:35.430 --> 00:58:39.270
So what you should do is imagine
this thing with wires on it,

00:58:39.270 --> 00:58:41.890
and vertical springs
that attach to it,

00:58:41.890 --> 00:58:44.260
and it's in mechanical
equilibrium.

00:58:44.260 --> 00:58:45.940
In least-squares,
that would be this.

00:58:45.940 --> 00:58:48.273
And someone say, whoa, how
did it get torqued out there?

00:58:48.273 --> 00:58:49.540
And you go, you just said it.

00:58:49.540 --> 00:58:50.980
That's exactly what happened.

00:58:50.980 --> 00:58:54.130
There's a giant
torque on that thing.

00:58:54.130 --> 00:58:55.000
Why?

00:58:55.000 --> 00:58:57.850
Because this is a spring, you
imagine a spring here pulling

00:58:57.850 --> 00:58:59.410
this, and it's pulling it up.

00:58:59.410 --> 00:59:02.680
And there's a spring over
here and it's pulling it down.

00:59:02.680 --> 00:59:03.850
Everybody got it?

00:59:03.850 --> 00:59:04.420
Right?

00:59:04.420 --> 00:59:06.280
And you say, yeah, there's
a bunch of stuff in here

00:59:06.280 --> 00:59:08.780
but they're just there, those
are just little displacements,

00:59:08.780 --> 00:59:11.530
they're putting a few
newtons here and there on it.

00:59:11.530 --> 00:59:13.300
Now let's talk about Huber.

00:59:13.300 --> 00:59:15.280
And let's do the
mechanical interpretation.

00:59:15.280 --> 00:59:17.770
Mechanical interpretation
is super interesting spring.

00:59:17.770 --> 00:59:20.590
It's a spring
where you expand it

00:59:20.590 --> 00:59:22.750
and the energy goes
up quadratically.

00:59:22.750 --> 00:59:26.950
But then you hit a point,
and after that, the energy

00:59:26.950 --> 00:59:27.670
grows linearly.

00:59:27.670 --> 00:59:29.950
And what that means is
it's a constant force.

00:59:29.950 --> 00:59:31.100
Everybody got that?

00:59:31.100 --> 00:59:34.720
And so what happens here
is that these springs

00:59:34.720 --> 00:59:37.360
are putting a force on this.

00:59:37.360 --> 00:59:40.300
But once you hit
the boundary, it's

00:59:40.300 --> 00:59:44.530
a constant force, not one
that continues to increase.

00:59:44.530 --> 00:59:46.810
OK, so again if that
helped you, fine.

00:59:46.810 --> 00:59:50.720
If it didn't, just completely
ignore it, that interpretation.

00:59:50.720 --> 00:59:51.950
So everybody got this?

00:59:51.950 --> 00:59:54.290
So anyway, what's obvious here--

00:59:54.290 --> 00:59:57.790
I mean this is like idiotic
points in R. You just

00:59:57.790 --> 00:59:59.363
plot things and whatever.

00:59:59.363 --> 01:00:01.780
In fact, for these things you
just draw a line through it,

01:00:01.780 --> 01:00:04.210
or it doesn't matter, right?

01:00:04.210 --> 01:00:06.110
What I can tell
you is this works--

01:00:06.110 --> 01:00:07.720
you get the same
thing in cases where

01:00:07.720 --> 01:00:09.940
it's not remotely obvious.

01:00:09.940 --> 01:00:13.750
So pretty much most times
people are fitting data,

01:00:13.750 --> 01:00:17.930
it should probably be done
with Huber, not least squares.

01:00:17.930 --> 01:00:20.230
So just pretty much.

01:00:20.230 --> 01:00:21.490
OK?

01:00:21.490 --> 01:00:25.300
That's-- in fact, there are
stunning examples of how well

01:00:25.300 --> 01:00:27.740
it just powers through outliers.

01:00:27.740 --> 01:00:28.240
Right?

01:00:28.240 --> 01:00:31.990
So there's examples where
20%, 30% of your data points

01:00:31.990 --> 01:00:35.470
are just crazy outliers,
and this thing just works.

01:00:35.470 --> 01:00:37.420
Everybody got this?

01:00:37.420 --> 01:00:38.930
So it's very cool.

01:00:38.930 --> 01:00:39.430
OK?

01:00:39.430 --> 01:00:42.550
So you know, and it's
named after Peter Huber,

01:00:42.550 --> 01:00:45.760
a statistician who
described the-- came up

01:00:45.760 --> 01:00:48.297
with the function but
reasoning from first principles

01:00:48.297 --> 01:00:50.380
about unknown distributions,
and blah, blah, blah.

01:00:50.380 --> 01:00:51.070
Right?

01:00:51.070 --> 01:00:51.910
But anyway.

01:00:51.910 --> 01:00:52.600
But that's our--

01:00:52.600 --> 01:00:55.180
So our amateur
interpretation is simply

01:00:55.180 --> 01:00:59.240
that he's using a
penalty that matches

01:00:59.240 --> 01:01:04.700
least squares for small things,
but then grows much more gently

01:01:04.700 --> 01:01:06.620
for large residuals.

01:01:06.620 --> 01:01:09.080
And another way to say
it is, it's cooler,

01:01:09.080 --> 01:01:11.960
it doesn't go crazy if there's
a large residual trying

01:01:11.960 --> 01:01:14.660
to reduce it and then you
get that kind of arrow.

01:01:14.660 --> 01:01:16.760
This-- sorry, this
torque on this thing.

01:01:16.760 --> 01:01:17.270
OK?

01:01:17.270 --> 01:01:18.230
Everybody got that?

01:01:21.020 --> 01:01:22.710
We'll look at
least-norm problems.

01:01:22.710 --> 01:01:26.180
So least-norm problems
are similar, right?

01:01:26.180 --> 01:01:29.240
Oh, I should mention every
time we look at these things,

01:01:29.240 --> 01:01:30.710
we look at simple versions.

01:01:30.710 --> 01:01:33.530
But here are some
things you know now.

01:01:33.530 --> 01:01:39.620
We could do-- here, go back to
this and do norm approximation.

01:01:39.620 --> 01:01:42.380
We can add convex constraints
and everything is cool.

01:01:42.380 --> 01:01:47.720
So for example, you could do
Huber monotonic regression.

01:01:47.720 --> 01:01:48.530
How about that?

01:01:48.530 --> 01:01:50.570
It's probably five
papers on that.

01:01:50.570 --> 01:01:53.690
OK so someone tell me what is
Huber monotonic regression?

01:01:57.190 --> 01:01:59.430
You know what I mean?

01:01:59.430 --> 01:02:02.760
You just do regression, use
a Huber penalty, number one.

01:02:02.760 --> 01:02:05.390
And then the other thing is,
you add the constraints, which

01:02:05.390 --> 01:02:07.670
is x1 is less than
x2 is less than x3

01:02:07.670 --> 01:02:09.590
is less-- everybody got that?

01:02:09.590 --> 01:02:12.530
OK, that is almost
certainly super

01:02:12.530 --> 01:02:13.820
useful in some application.

01:02:13.820 --> 01:02:15.440
Everybody got it?

01:02:15.440 --> 01:02:18.057
And so you could
anthropomorphize

01:02:18.057 --> 01:02:18.890
that and everything.

01:02:18.890 --> 01:02:24.050
So we would-- how
would we say it?

01:02:24.050 --> 01:02:27.350
You just say, it's a
[INAUDIBLE] or something, right?

01:02:27.350 --> 01:02:29.000
It's like, it's just nothing.

01:02:29.000 --> 01:02:30.590
Like we know how to solve that.

01:02:30.590 --> 01:02:31.520
Right?

01:02:31.520 --> 01:02:34.310
And then they would say, oh,
I know a very fancy algorithm

01:02:34.310 --> 01:02:34.910
for solving.

01:02:34.910 --> 01:02:36.260
You just say, stop.

01:02:36.260 --> 01:02:37.370
Stop right there.

01:02:37.370 --> 01:02:39.590
Just don't say anything
else because you're just

01:02:39.590 --> 01:02:41.630
embarrassing yourself, right?

01:02:41.630 --> 01:02:43.580
We know 50 ways to
solve it and all of them

01:02:43.580 --> 01:02:44.540
are better than yours.

01:02:44.540 --> 01:02:46.580
So anyway just
leave it that way.

01:02:46.580 --> 01:02:47.130
OK.

01:02:47.130 --> 01:02:47.630
All right.

01:02:47.630 --> 01:02:49.290
So I'm not saying
stuff like that.

01:02:49.290 --> 01:02:51.200
So you can complicate these
things as much as you like,

01:02:51.200 --> 01:02:53.510
or you can add non-negative
and OK, blah, blah, blah.

01:02:53.510 --> 01:02:54.120
OK fine.

01:02:54.120 --> 01:02:57.440
So let's go here,
least-norm problems.

01:02:57.440 --> 01:03:00.890
Here, you're minimizing a
norm, subject x equals b.

01:03:00.890 --> 01:03:07.730
I mean it's the projection
of the solution set

01:03:07.730 --> 01:03:09.920
of a x equals b onto the origin.

01:03:09.920 --> 01:03:10.640
Right?

01:03:10.640 --> 01:03:12.830
And if you want to project
it onto some other thing,

01:03:12.830 --> 01:03:15.650
yeah go ahead and put an x
minus and then some target.

01:03:15.650 --> 01:03:17.690
Right?

01:03:17.690 --> 01:03:21.750
In estimation, this says I have
perfect measurements or really,

01:03:21.750 --> 01:03:25.090
really good measurements but
I don't have enough of them.

01:03:25.090 --> 01:03:28.500
And so in that case,
you'd say, well, yeah, no.

01:03:28.500 --> 01:03:34.230
I actually-- I have temperature
sensors on that chip.

01:03:34.230 --> 01:03:37.380
And you say, how good are they?

01:03:37.380 --> 01:03:38.700
And you go, oh, they're fine.

01:03:38.700 --> 01:03:39.837
More than we need.

01:03:39.837 --> 01:03:41.670
And you'd say, well
then what's the problem?

01:03:41.670 --> 01:03:42.690
And you'd say,
well, the problem is

01:03:42.690 --> 01:03:44.790
I'd like to know what is the
temperature at other points

01:03:44.790 --> 01:03:46.415
on the chip where
I'm not measuring it?

01:03:46.415 --> 01:03:47.670
Everybody following this?

01:03:47.670 --> 01:03:49.380
That's the problem.

01:03:49.380 --> 01:03:52.890
And that's the case where a is--

01:03:52.890 --> 01:03:56.430
x is going to be the
temperature at all the points.

01:03:56.430 --> 01:04:00.900
Ax equals b is going
to be telling you

01:04:00.900 --> 01:04:03.533
that these are the measurements
you would get at those points--

01:04:03.533 --> 01:04:04.950
this agrees with
your measurements

01:04:04.950 --> 01:04:07.020
but you don't have
enough to determine x.

01:04:07.020 --> 01:04:08.940
And so the question
is, what's x?

01:04:08.940 --> 01:04:12.523
And so then you'd say, well, I
need a measure of plausibility

01:04:12.523 --> 01:04:14.940
and the measure of plausibility
here is that the norm of x

01:04:14.940 --> 01:04:15.690
is small.

01:04:15.690 --> 01:04:17.340
Right?

01:04:17.340 --> 01:04:18.570
Then that's estimation.

01:04:18.570 --> 01:04:21.330
And in design, this would be--

01:04:21.330 --> 01:04:25.550
you'd think of these as
just constraints, right?

01:04:25.550 --> 01:04:27.192
Or requirements,
and then you want

01:04:27.192 --> 01:04:28.400
to minimize the norm of this.

01:04:28.400 --> 01:04:34.160
So classic one is for
example, minimum fuel motion

01:04:34.160 --> 01:04:36.270
from one place to another.

01:04:36.270 --> 01:04:36.770
Right?

01:04:36.770 --> 01:04:38.750
So move my satellite
back where it's supposed

01:04:38.750 --> 01:04:40.100
to be, minimize the fuel.

01:04:40.100 --> 01:04:41.660
That perfect example.

01:04:41.660 --> 01:04:43.430
Right?

01:04:43.430 --> 01:04:44.408
Or, I don't know.

01:04:44.408 --> 01:04:45.950
It could be all
sorts of other stuff.

01:04:45.950 --> 01:04:46.860
Anyway, that's it.

01:04:46.860 --> 01:04:48.940
Everybody got this?

01:04:48.940 --> 01:04:53.350
So these are examples of just
high level least-norm problems.

01:04:53.350 --> 01:04:57.991
And least-squares again,
it's something you know.

01:04:57.991 --> 01:05:00.400
You can do sum of
absolute values,

01:05:00.400 --> 01:05:02.110
as I've mentioned
several times, that's

01:05:02.110 --> 01:05:07.310
an elementary model of
fuel use in some cases.

01:05:07.310 --> 01:05:09.190
You can also do a
least penalty problem.

01:05:09.190 --> 01:05:10.670
Like this, which is really cool.

01:05:10.670 --> 01:05:12.970
So you'd say, here's my penalty.

01:05:12.970 --> 01:05:14.440
That would actually
be super useful

01:05:14.440 --> 01:05:17.050
because you'd actually go
and ask somebody, tell me

01:05:17.050 --> 01:05:18.130
about your thrusters.

01:05:18.130 --> 01:05:21.610
And they would say, oh, whoa,
they're super sophisticated

01:05:21.610 --> 01:05:23.487
and they'd give you
a booklet this thick.

01:05:23.487 --> 01:05:25.570
You could just read this
and you'll understand it,

01:05:25.570 --> 01:05:26.770
it depends on the
fuel, and blah, blah,

01:05:26.770 --> 01:05:29.020
blah, go on and on very
sophisticated, right?

01:05:29.020 --> 01:05:30.760
And then you'd say,
well to produce

01:05:30.760 --> 01:05:33.670
a certain number of newtons,
what's your fuel use?

01:05:33.670 --> 01:05:34.307
Right?

01:05:34.307 --> 01:05:35.890
And then they would
say, oh, yeah, no.

01:05:35.890 --> 01:05:37.270
There's a whole sequence
of papers on that

01:05:37.270 --> 01:05:38.812
and it'd be unbelievably
complicated.

01:05:38.812 --> 01:05:40.520
I wouldn't understand
any of it anything.

01:05:40.520 --> 01:05:43.180
But at the end of the day, you'd
say can you draw me a picture?

01:05:43.180 --> 01:05:43.840
Right?

01:05:43.840 --> 01:05:46.570
And it would almost
certainly be convex.

01:05:46.570 --> 01:05:47.230
Right?

01:05:47.230 --> 01:05:48.770
And that would be your penalty.

01:05:48.770 --> 01:05:51.570
And then you'd say,
then when you do this,

01:05:51.570 --> 01:05:53.060
you're actually
literally doing--

01:05:53.060 --> 01:05:55.640
you're doing something very
close to actual minimum

01:05:55.640 --> 01:05:58.670
fuel trajectory design
or something like that.

01:05:58.670 --> 01:06:00.710
Everybody got this?

01:06:00.710 --> 01:06:02.240
So that's the picture.

01:06:05.700 --> 01:06:07.380
OK.

01:06:07.380 --> 01:06:09.270
Now you can do
regularized approximation,

01:06:09.270 --> 01:06:10.853
and these are ideas
we're going to see

01:06:10.853 --> 01:06:13.680
in lots of other contexts
like statistical and others.

01:06:13.680 --> 01:06:18.040
But for now-- so here you
have a bi-criterion problem.

01:06:18.040 --> 01:06:20.558
So if you like, it's a
vector optimization problem

01:06:20.558 --> 01:06:22.350
with-- if you want to
be pedantic about it,

01:06:22.350 --> 01:06:24.810
you could say, with
over the cone r

01:06:24.810 --> 01:06:26.610
plus squared or something.

01:06:26.610 --> 01:06:28.270
And so that looks like this.

01:06:28.270 --> 01:06:30.930
You'd say, you know, I really
want ax minus b to be small,

01:06:30.930 --> 01:06:33.290
but I also want x to be small.

01:06:33.290 --> 01:06:36.380
So that's what this says.

01:06:36.380 --> 01:06:41.660
And estimation is--

01:06:41.660 --> 01:06:44.000
I mean, we'll get into
a lot of this later.

01:06:44.000 --> 01:06:46.940
But that could have a
linear measurement model,

01:06:46.940 --> 01:06:50.570
y equals ax plus v, and
you could say, yeah,

01:06:50.570 --> 01:06:51.530
the noise here--

01:06:51.530 --> 01:06:53.360
I mean, my prior
is that it's small.

01:06:53.360 --> 01:06:55.730
So it's small in
some-- in this norm.

01:06:55.730 --> 01:06:57.870
These could even be
different norms, by the way.

01:06:57.870 --> 01:07:00.830
In fact, they often are
in applications, right?

01:07:00.830 --> 01:07:02.420
Then you'd say, oh,
yeah, but I also

01:07:02.420 --> 01:07:04.380
have a prior that x is small.

01:07:04.380 --> 01:07:06.650
And then that would
be this part here.

01:07:09.280 --> 01:07:09.910
Yeah.

01:07:09.910 --> 01:07:14.070
So here for example,
if we were going to--

01:07:14.070 --> 01:07:19.150
well, socially non-positive
but suppose I was going to work

01:07:19.150 --> 01:07:19.840
on--

01:07:19.840 --> 01:07:22.330
control a missile
heading towards a target.

01:07:22.330 --> 01:07:24.340
And someone would say,
well, what are you--

01:07:24.340 --> 01:07:28.990
this thing is going to be
at the end, how close you

01:07:28.990 --> 01:07:30.520
are to the target?

01:07:30.520 --> 01:07:32.230
And so, I'd say,
how does that work?

01:07:32.230 --> 01:07:35.540
And they go, well, OK,
it wouldn't-- it depends.

01:07:35.540 --> 01:07:36.040
Right?

01:07:36.040 --> 01:07:38.560
Now they're shaped charges
and things get like insane.

01:07:38.560 --> 01:07:39.970
But let's ignore that.

01:07:39.970 --> 01:07:41.922
And you just say, well
my kill probability--

01:07:41.922 --> 01:07:43.630
this would be the
things they would say--

01:07:43.630 --> 01:07:47.825
depends on the inverse square
of the distance from the target

01:07:47.825 --> 01:07:48.700
and blah, blah, blah.

01:07:48.700 --> 01:07:51.070
This would be old-school
non-shaped charges, right?

01:07:51.070 --> 01:07:54.580
And that would tell you that
this should be a norm in R3.

01:07:54.580 --> 01:07:55.120
Right?

01:07:55.120 --> 01:07:58.390
It's how close do you get.

01:07:58.390 --> 01:08:01.720
And then, the norm here
could be something else.

01:08:01.720 --> 01:08:03.990
Right?

01:08:03.990 --> 01:08:05.740
By the way that would
be a really good use

01:08:05.740 --> 01:08:08.330
for a penalty right there.

01:08:08.330 --> 01:08:12.050
As long as we're on a
socially non-positive topic.

01:08:12.050 --> 01:08:14.000
So here's one, ready?

01:08:14.000 --> 01:08:18.350
You'd say, well
what's your penalty?

01:08:18.350 --> 01:08:22.380
And you say, oh, if you get
within 10 meters, that's fine,

01:08:22.380 --> 01:08:23.069
thank you.

01:08:23.069 --> 01:08:24.361
And you go, how about 2 meters?

01:08:24.361 --> 01:08:26.420
And you go, ha, it's
not going to matter.

01:08:26.420 --> 01:08:28.950
You get within 10, then
that's good enough.

01:08:28.950 --> 01:08:29.810
Thank you very much.

01:08:29.810 --> 01:08:30.310
OK?

01:08:30.310 --> 01:08:31.890
So that's-- anyway.

01:08:31.890 --> 01:08:34.729
And by the way, if you
translate that into a penalty,

01:08:34.729 --> 01:08:36.662
what does that
sound like to you?

01:08:36.662 --> 01:08:37.529
[INAUDIBLE]

01:08:37.529 --> 01:08:38.270
What is it?

01:08:38.270 --> 01:08:39.260
Deadzone [INAUDIBLE].

01:08:39.260 --> 01:08:42.109
It says-- in that case, it
is a deadzone, literally.

01:08:42.109 --> 01:08:42.649
Thank you.

01:08:42.649 --> 01:08:43.149
Yeah.

01:08:43.149 --> 01:08:43.670
OK, good.

01:08:43.670 --> 01:08:44.450
Sorry about this.

01:08:44.450 --> 01:08:45.710
But sorry, I don't know
how we got on to this,

01:08:45.710 --> 01:08:47.090
but it just happened, right?

01:08:47.090 --> 01:08:52.010
OK, and what if I tell you
that if you're more than 50--

01:08:52.010 --> 01:08:54.770
100 meters away, then you
might as well have not even

01:08:54.770 --> 01:08:56.090
fired the missile.

01:08:56.090 --> 01:08:58.694
What does that sound like?

01:08:58.694 --> 01:08:59.510
[INAUDIBLE]

01:08:59.510 --> 01:09:00.140
What's that?

01:09:00.140 --> 01:09:00.830
[INAUDIBLE]

01:09:00.830 --> 01:09:03.380
Yeah, it could be some kind of--
it just means basically like

01:09:03.380 --> 01:09:04.310
don't even--

01:09:04.310 --> 01:09:06.455
like, you could actually
literally put it--

01:09:06.455 --> 01:09:07.880
yeah, with some kind of barrier.

01:09:07.880 --> 01:09:10.550
Say, if you don't think
according to your model,

01:09:10.550 --> 01:09:12.710
you can get within 100
meters, and you know what?

01:09:12.710 --> 01:09:13.550
Don't even waste it.

01:09:13.550 --> 01:09:15.258
They're expensive or
something like that.

01:09:15.258 --> 01:09:16.010
OK?

01:09:16.010 --> 01:09:19.430
This is just an example of
where these kinds of discussions

01:09:19.430 --> 01:09:20.060
come up.

01:09:20.060 --> 01:09:20.630
OK?

01:09:20.630 --> 01:09:21.574
So OK.

01:09:24.755 --> 01:09:26.130
A very interesting
interpretation

01:09:26.130 --> 01:09:29.290
of this that we're going to come
back to later is super cool.

01:09:29.290 --> 01:09:30.420
It's basically this.

01:09:30.420 --> 01:09:32.970
Someone says, why do you want--
when you make this estimate,

01:09:32.970 --> 01:09:33.970
why do you want x small?

01:09:33.970 --> 01:09:35.470
Do you have a prior
that x is small?

01:09:35.470 --> 01:09:36.390
And you're like, no.

01:09:36.390 --> 01:09:37.200
I don't.

01:09:37.200 --> 01:09:39.310
Then you go, but why
do you want x small?

01:09:39.310 --> 01:09:40.727
The reason would be this.

01:09:40.727 --> 01:09:42.060
Here's another very good reason.

01:09:42.060 --> 01:09:45.510
You say, I don't really
know a that well.

01:09:45.510 --> 01:09:46.859
You go, OK, cool.

01:09:46.859 --> 01:09:49.600
I mean, that's actually true in
almost any application, right?

01:09:49.600 --> 01:09:51.160
I don't know a that well.

01:09:51.160 --> 01:09:54.300
And you'd say, so what's this
thing about x being small?

01:09:54.300 --> 01:09:57.090
And you go, well,
if you don't know--

01:09:57.090 --> 01:10:00.522
if a can vary, if
a can wiggle around

01:10:00.522 --> 01:10:02.980
and you don't know it that
much, let me ask you a question,

01:10:02.980 --> 01:10:07.020
how much would that matter
if you chose x equals 0?

01:10:07.020 --> 01:10:08.490
Not at all.

01:10:08.490 --> 01:10:13.840
If you made x really big,
and you have a x, and then

01:10:13.840 --> 01:10:17.460
a varies, what's going
to happen to a x?

01:10:17.460 --> 01:10:17.960
Big.

01:10:17.960 --> 01:10:19.110
What's that?

01:10:19.110 --> 01:10:19.610
Big.

01:10:19.610 --> 01:10:22.170
OK, so what it says,
is the following.

01:10:22.170 --> 01:10:24.500
You might want x small
and someone say why?

01:10:24.500 --> 01:10:27.350
And you'd say because, look,
in my model, x multiplies a.

01:10:27.350 --> 01:10:28.910
And you go, so?

01:10:28.910 --> 01:10:32.230
And you go, well, look at this.

01:10:32.230 --> 01:10:38.320
If a varies, since it
multiplies x, the smaller x is,

01:10:38.320 --> 01:10:41.560
the less effect a
varying has on a x.

01:10:41.560 --> 01:10:43.540
Again this is
unbelievable-- don't

01:10:43.540 --> 01:10:45.250
try to-- don't make
this sophisticated.

01:10:45.250 --> 01:10:48.230
What I'm saying it's
dumb as what I'm saying.

01:10:48.230 --> 01:10:49.300
Everybody got this?

01:10:49.300 --> 01:10:52.450
So that's another very
good reason why you want--

01:10:52.450 --> 01:10:57.190
you might want x to be
small because it becomes--

01:10:57.190 --> 01:10:59.720
a x becomes less sensitive.

01:10:59.720 --> 01:11:00.220
OK?

01:11:00.220 --> 01:11:01.985
We will see lots
about this later.

01:11:01.985 --> 01:11:02.485
OK?

01:11:05.730 --> 01:11:07.290
All right.

01:11:07.290 --> 01:11:09.460
So how do you solve
these kinds of problems?

01:11:09.460 --> 01:11:13.110
Well, again we've seen this
before in the general context.

01:11:13.110 --> 01:11:15.360
You scalarize the
problem, right?

01:11:15.360 --> 01:11:20.065
And many ways to do
this is if you just

01:11:20.065 --> 01:11:21.690
apply the most direct
way, you do this.

01:11:21.690 --> 01:11:24.240
You refer to this as
your primary objective,

01:11:24.240 --> 01:11:25.620
and gamma is--

01:11:25.620 --> 01:11:27.180
this is your
secondary objective.

01:11:27.180 --> 01:11:31.350
And gamma weights the primary--
and actually, it literally

01:11:31.350 --> 01:11:34.200
weights the secondary objective
relative to the first.

01:11:34.200 --> 01:11:35.610
That's what gamma is here.

01:11:35.610 --> 01:11:38.610
People actually typically
don't solve this.

01:11:38.610 --> 01:11:41.070
Instead, they would do
something like that.

01:11:41.070 --> 01:11:42.810
And this has lots
of names depending

01:11:42.810 --> 01:11:43.900
on who you hang out with.

01:11:43.900 --> 01:11:45.870
But it's Tikhonov
regularization.

01:11:45.870 --> 01:11:48.900
I guess in Western statistics,
it's called ridge regression,

01:11:48.900 --> 01:11:51.960
I think, and it's got
probably has other names.

01:11:51.960 --> 01:11:53.820
Like, I don't know.

01:11:53.820 --> 01:11:55.320
OK.

01:11:55.320 --> 01:11:59.790
And it basically-- it says,
I want a x minus b small

01:11:59.790 --> 01:12:00.600
and 2 norm squared.

01:12:00.600 --> 01:12:02.640
I mean, this has an
immediate solution.

01:12:02.640 --> 01:12:05.200
It's like this thing, right?

01:12:05.200 --> 01:12:06.830
But that would be it.

01:12:06.830 --> 01:12:09.550
And we'll look at some
interpretations of what that is

01:12:09.550 --> 01:12:11.020
and all that sort
of stuff later.

01:12:11.020 --> 01:12:13.540
OK.

01:12:13.540 --> 01:12:14.040
All right.

01:12:14.040 --> 01:12:15.870
So our next example
also is just going

01:12:15.870 --> 01:12:19.320
to show you, this a baby
example of how you would--

01:12:19.320 --> 01:12:20.970
how you fiddle with the weights.

01:12:20.970 --> 01:12:22.050
Right?

01:12:22.050 --> 01:12:25.243
So actually if you're doing
statistical stuff, how do you--

01:12:25.243 --> 01:12:27.160
someone tell me how do
you choose the weights?

01:12:27.160 --> 01:12:31.167
How do you choose like
let's say delta here?

01:12:31.167 --> 01:12:33.250
And if you're fitting a
statistical model, I mean,

01:12:33.250 --> 01:12:34.030
we'll talk about this later.

01:12:34.030 --> 01:12:35.410
But how do you actually do it?

01:12:38.080 --> 01:12:39.170
There are wrong answers.

01:12:39.170 --> 01:12:39.670
Let's see.

01:12:39.670 --> 01:12:40.060
What--

01:12:40.060 --> 01:12:40.808
Cross-validation.

01:12:40.808 --> 01:12:41.350
What is that?

01:12:41.350 --> 01:12:42.070
Cross-validation.

01:12:42.070 --> 01:12:42.817
Ah, beautiful.

01:12:42.817 --> 01:12:43.900
That was the right answer.

01:12:43.900 --> 01:12:44.410
Good.

01:12:44.410 --> 01:12:45.430
Yeah.

01:12:45.430 --> 01:12:47.770
The wrong answer was to
launch into some long theory

01:12:47.770 --> 01:12:50.170
about Gaussian and
asymptotic this and that,

01:12:50.170 --> 01:12:51.307
and blabber on and on.

01:12:51.307 --> 01:12:52.390
That was the wrong answer.

01:12:52.390 --> 01:12:54.370
Even though those are
interesting observations,

01:12:54.370 --> 01:12:56.140
and that's the right answer.

01:12:56.140 --> 01:12:59.050
OK, so it was
cross-validation by the way,

01:12:59.050 --> 01:13:00.670
or just out of
sample validation.

01:13:00.670 --> 01:13:01.180
OK.

01:13:01.180 --> 01:13:02.710
We'll get to that later.

01:13:02.710 --> 01:13:06.340
OK, so this is another example.

01:13:06.340 --> 01:13:09.250
By the way, a lot of
times the scalarization

01:13:09.250 --> 01:13:13.550
is actually done by people
and it's completely intuitive.

01:13:13.550 --> 01:13:14.050
Right?

01:13:14.050 --> 01:13:17.050
Like you'd say, I want
efficiency but also

01:13:17.050 --> 01:13:18.505
ride quality.

01:13:18.505 --> 01:13:20.380
And you're like, well,
how do they trade off?

01:13:20.380 --> 01:13:20.830
And you go, I don't know.

01:13:20.830 --> 01:13:22.030
Show me some choices.

01:13:22.030 --> 01:13:23.870
And then somebody
just makes a decision.

01:13:23.870 --> 01:13:24.370
Right?

01:13:24.370 --> 01:13:27.250
So you don't have something
as simple and obvious

01:13:27.250 --> 01:13:29.770
and defensible as
cross-validation there.

01:13:29.770 --> 01:13:30.370
Right?

01:13:30.370 --> 01:13:32.620
So I mean, I guess you
could do weird experiments.

01:13:32.620 --> 01:13:36.430
You could put people in the car,
and they could finish a trip,

01:13:36.430 --> 01:13:37.390
and go, whoa.

01:13:37.390 --> 01:13:39.500
Oh, my back really
hurts, but wow.

01:13:39.500 --> 01:13:41.780
It didn't cost very much,
the energy for that trip.

01:13:41.780 --> 01:13:44.330
Anyway, you can't do that.

01:13:44.330 --> 01:13:45.150
I don't think.

01:13:45.150 --> 01:13:45.650
Right?

01:13:45.650 --> 01:13:48.660
So that's not how it works.

01:13:48.660 --> 01:13:49.160
OK.

01:13:49.160 --> 01:13:50.600
But this will be an example.

01:13:50.600 --> 01:13:52.430
In a lot of areas like--

01:13:52.430 --> 01:13:54.620
unfortunately, in some
areas like image processing,

01:13:54.620 --> 01:13:55.490
it's endemic.

01:13:55.490 --> 01:13:58.940
Where people just turn knobs
until they like what they see.

01:13:58.940 --> 01:13:59.900
So it's fine.

01:13:59.900 --> 01:14:01.200
I'm not making fun of it.

01:14:03.730 --> 01:14:07.557
Control is another one right
where they just make stuff up.

01:14:07.557 --> 01:14:08.140
And it's fine.

01:14:08.140 --> 01:14:10.330
They turn knobs and then
they like what they see.

01:14:10.330 --> 01:14:11.740
So anyway.

01:14:11.740 --> 01:14:12.700
All right.

01:14:12.700 --> 01:14:15.290
So we'll do-- this is
kind of a classic problem.

01:14:15.290 --> 01:14:17.000
It's like optimal input design.

01:14:17.000 --> 01:14:19.730
So I have an input,
which is in this case,

01:14:19.730 --> 01:14:23.050
it's a time sequence,
u, and what comes out

01:14:23.050 --> 01:14:25.390
is another time sequence,
y, and they're just

01:14:25.390 --> 01:14:26.600
related by convolution.

01:14:26.600 --> 01:14:29.770
So this would be called,
in dialect, a linear in EE

01:14:29.770 --> 01:14:33.372
or something, ME dialect,
it's a linear dynamical system

01:14:33.372 --> 01:14:34.330
or something like that.

01:14:34.330 --> 01:14:34.830
Right?

01:14:34.830 --> 01:14:35.840
It's a linear system.

01:14:35.840 --> 01:14:37.840
And I think in
math, you would say

01:14:37.840 --> 01:14:40.810
it's a convolution
kernel with this--

01:14:40.810 --> 01:14:43.570
h is the convolution kernel and
this is just the convolution.

01:14:43.570 --> 01:14:44.680
Right?

01:14:44.680 --> 01:14:46.150
So here's your job.

01:14:49.260 --> 01:14:51.700
You got a couple of objectives.

01:14:51.700 --> 01:14:54.130
The first one is you care
about the tracking error.

01:14:54.130 --> 01:14:55.770
So there's some
desired trajectory--

01:14:55.770 --> 01:14:57.750
you want the output
to do something.

01:14:57.750 --> 01:14:58.840
I don't know what it is.

01:14:58.840 --> 01:15:02.010
Maybe it's just being constant,
or go to a certain value,

01:15:02.010 --> 01:15:03.780
or maybe it follows
some pattern.

01:15:03.780 --> 01:15:05.730
That's this one.

01:15:05.730 --> 01:15:08.100
And this would be called
your tracking error.

01:15:08.100 --> 01:15:10.837
At the same time, you want the
input magnitude to be small.

01:15:13.440 --> 01:15:17.820
And I'm just using the L2
norm squared because why not?

01:15:17.820 --> 01:15:20.100
It's traditional and so on.

01:15:20.100 --> 01:15:21.960
And you also might
want the input

01:15:21.960 --> 01:15:24.390
that you're going to
apply to be smooth,

01:15:24.390 --> 01:15:28.740
and so you take the sum of the
squares of the differences.

01:15:28.740 --> 01:15:30.780
Something like that.

01:15:30.780 --> 01:15:31.350
OK.

01:15:31.350 --> 01:15:34.710
And you'd end up-- then you
form a regularized least-squares

01:15:34.710 --> 01:15:36.580
problem and it would
look like this.

01:15:36.580 --> 01:15:40.950
So you'd say my tracking
error is my first--

01:15:40.950 --> 01:15:43.950
is my primary objective.

01:15:43.950 --> 01:15:48.250
My secondary and tertiary
objectives are maybe

01:15:48.250 --> 01:15:49.360
I want this--

01:15:49.360 --> 01:15:51.850
I want the input small, and
I want it not too wiggly.

01:15:51.850 --> 01:15:52.510
There you go.

01:15:52.510 --> 01:15:54.760
And then the numbers,
delta and eta,

01:15:54.760 --> 01:15:58.450
here, are the ones that
tell you how much you

01:15:58.450 --> 01:16:01.827
care about the input
being small and/or wiggly,

01:16:01.827 --> 01:16:03.160
compared to your tracking error.

01:16:03.160 --> 01:16:04.390
Right?

01:16:04.390 --> 01:16:06.227
By the way, hilariously,
the same problem

01:16:06.227 --> 01:16:08.560
comes up not just in control
and mechanical engineering,

01:16:08.560 --> 01:16:11.230
you get exactly the same
thing like in finance.

01:16:11.230 --> 01:16:14.230
Like you'd say-- like
your primary one might be,

01:16:14.230 --> 01:16:17.290
I need to track this bond
portfolio plus or minus

01:16:17.290 --> 01:16:19.300
30 basis points, or
something like that.

01:16:19.300 --> 01:16:22.690
And this would be like, I want--
this would be like-- in fact,

01:16:22.690 --> 01:16:24.700
instead of the derivative,
you'd literally have,

01:16:24.700 --> 01:16:28.670
what's-- you literally have
what they'd call the turnover,

01:16:28.670 --> 01:16:31.150
which would be the
amount of trading you do.

01:16:31.150 --> 01:16:32.320
Right?

01:16:32.320 --> 01:16:34.570
This would have-- the
magnitude would mean something

01:16:34.570 --> 01:16:35.360
else entirely.

01:16:35.360 --> 01:16:37.060
But the point is,
this problem is

01:16:37.060 --> 01:16:40.750
repeated in 50 different fields,
or problems very like this.

01:16:40.750 --> 01:16:41.270
OK.

01:16:41.270 --> 01:16:41.770
All right.

01:16:41.770 --> 01:16:42.700
So this is the idea.

01:16:42.700 --> 01:16:44.380
And it's just a
least-squares problem.

01:16:44.380 --> 01:16:46.300
I mean, later, we're
going to come back

01:16:46.300 --> 01:16:49.450
and I'm going to swap
these for other penalties,

01:16:49.450 --> 01:16:51.490
and I'm going to ask
you, like what do you

01:16:51.490 --> 01:16:52.610
think is going to happen?

01:16:52.610 --> 01:16:53.110
OK?

01:16:53.110 --> 01:16:54.880
But for now, they're not.

01:16:54.880 --> 01:16:57.280
And this is what
it might look like,

01:16:57.280 --> 01:16:58.870
and it's just-- this
is just silly too

01:16:58.870 --> 01:17:01.370
but just to see how this looks.

01:17:01.370 --> 01:17:04.330
So at the top, delta is
0 but I totally forgot.

01:17:04.330 --> 01:17:06.520
Delta penalizes the derivative.

01:17:06.520 --> 01:17:08.440
OK?

01:17:08.440 --> 01:17:12.790
So in the top, delta is
small, and-- oh, by the way,

01:17:12.790 --> 01:17:15.670
what I want to track
is, I'll tell you some--

01:17:15.670 --> 01:17:18.970
that thing, here's
the dialect for that.

01:17:18.970 --> 01:17:21.360
That would be like
a square wave.

01:17:21.360 --> 01:17:24.340
And I think I've heard it in
control called something else.

01:17:24.340 --> 01:17:26.560
It's like-- it's a
specific maneuver,

01:17:26.560 --> 01:17:29.440
like, I want this
thing to go here,

01:17:29.440 --> 01:17:31.380
and then later go
there, and then go back.

01:17:31.380 --> 01:17:33.610
So there's even a name
for that, which I forget.

01:17:33.610 --> 01:17:34.870
But it doesn't matter.

01:17:34.870 --> 01:17:37.450
OK?

01:17:37.450 --> 01:17:38.862
So that's what
you want it to do.

01:17:38.862 --> 01:17:39.820
And here's what's cool.

01:17:39.820 --> 01:17:41.140
Here's an input you create.

01:17:41.140 --> 01:17:42.760
It looks like this, right?

01:17:42.760 --> 01:17:44.710
And you know, look at that.

01:17:44.710 --> 01:17:46.780
It actually tracks
it like really well.

01:17:46.780 --> 01:17:49.270
I mean, that's what
this math is for, right?

01:17:49.270 --> 01:17:51.520
So it does that.

01:17:51.520 --> 01:17:53.290
And you can give all
sorts of-- obviously

01:17:53.290 --> 01:17:57.880
the system here has some
oscillatory stuff in it

01:17:57.880 --> 01:17:59.290
to make this interesting.

01:17:59.290 --> 01:18:03.220
And you could-- people would
give all sorts of crazy stories

01:18:03.220 --> 01:18:04.420
about what's happening here.

01:18:04.420 --> 01:18:06.337
Like, I have friends in
mechanical engineering

01:18:06.337 --> 01:18:08.250
who would say, oh
that's very clever.

01:18:08.250 --> 01:18:09.320
And you go, what is this?

01:18:09.320 --> 01:18:11.900
And you say, well,
you're exciting,

01:18:11.900 --> 01:18:14.390
these high frequency
modes in this thing,

01:18:14.390 --> 01:18:18.590
in just the right way so that
when the real thing goes down,

01:18:18.590 --> 01:18:21.238
you won't excite those modes and
blah, blah, they go on and on.

01:18:21.238 --> 01:18:22.280
I mean it kind of makes--

01:18:22.280 --> 01:18:24.440
I mean, and they appear to
know what they're talking about

01:18:24.440 --> 01:18:25.523
amongst each other, right?

01:18:25.523 --> 01:18:26.840
So it's fine.

01:18:26.840 --> 01:18:29.570
But I'm just saying
they would look at that

01:18:29.570 --> 01:18:31.910
and explain it, right?

01:18:31.910 --> 01:18:32.510
OK.

01:18:32.510 --> 01:18:35.030
So what happens is
you say, no, no, no.

01:18:35.030 --> 01:18:37.040
This input is too wiggly.

01:18:37.040 --> 01:18:41.900
So what we're going to
do, actually it's too big.

01:18:41.900 --> 01:18:44.438
Because, look, it goes
down to minus 9 and plus 5.

01:18:44.438 --> 01:18:46.730
And you'd say, so what I'm
going to do in the middle is

01:18:46.730 --> 01:18:47.690
I'm going to put--

01:18:47.690 --> 01:18:49.760
I'm still-- I don't
care about wiggliness,

01:18:49.760 --> 01:18:51.410
but I'm going to turn up AIDA.

01:18:51.410 --> 01:18:53.980
What AIDA will do is it's
going to increase your tracking

01:18:53.980 --> 01:18:54.480
error.

01:18:54.480 --> 01:18:57.630
You can see that over here, that
you're not tracking as well.

01:18:57.630 --> 01:19:00.290
But now you have an input that's
a lot-- you know, whatever.

01:19:00.290 --> 01:19:03.800
It's like three times smaller,
roughly, order of magnitude,

01:19:03.800 --> 01:19:04.490
right?

01:19:04.490 --> 01:19:07.190
So that would be the idea.

01:19:07.190 --> 01:19:09.280
And then, finally,
you would say,

01:19:09.280 --> 01:19:11.498
let's crank up the smoothness.

01:19:11.498 --> 01:19:13.540
And you would get something
that looks like that,

01:19:13.540 --> 01:19:16.000
and that's a nice smooth
input, and this is

01:19:16.000 --> 01:19:17.680
the tracking error you'd get.

01:19:17.680 --> 01:19:20.530
And then how would this actually
work if you were doing this?

01:19:20.530 --> 01:19:23.170
Probably people would
look at it and go, no,

01:19:23.170 --> 01:19:26.032
tracking error is too big
here and you back off,

01:19:26.032 --> 01:19:26.740
one or the other.

01:19:26.740 --> 01:19:27.760
And you say, no,
it's too wiggly.

01:19:27.760 --> 01:19:28.930
No, it's too big, too small.

01:19:28.930 --> 01:19:30.597
And you go back and
forth until you were

01:19:30.597 --> 01:19:32.890
either bored, or satisfied.

01:19:32.890 --> 01:19:33.610
Right?

01:19:33.610 --> 01:19:35.110
So that's-- right.

01:19:35.110 --> 01:19:40.000
That's called
hyperparameter tweaking.

01:19:40.000 --> 01:19:42.590
If you think it's bad
in machine learning,

01:19:42.590 --> 01:19:44.440
it's really bad in control.

01:19:44.440 --> 01:19:46.390
But that's fine.

01:19:46.390 --> 01:19:47.548
OK, everybody got this?

01:19:47.548 --> 01:19:49.340
I mean, this is all
kind of obvious, right?

01:19:49.340 --> 01:19:52.010
I think, here.

01:19:52.010 --> 01:19:54.370
So I think what we'll
do is-- actually,

01:19:54.370 --> 01:19:56.110
I think we're
going to quit here,

01:19:56.110 --> 01:19:59.670
and we'll continue on Thursday.

